#h1{Barkdown: a static site generator for brashtag}

Barkdown turns a folder of posts written in brashtag notation into a website that can be copied to any static
host as it is: GitHub Pages, Netlify, an S3 bucket, or a directory served by nginx. It is a single Go program
with no dependencies outside the standard library. The only foreign code is the brashtag parser itself, about
two hundred lines, which is included here unchanged.

It takes its name from the original barkdown, `cmd/barkdown` in the brashtag repository: a forty-line
converter that reads one document on standard input and prints HTML. The original shows the whole idea in
miniature: parse the text into a tree of bags, walk the tree, and turn each tag into an HTML element. This
barkdown keeps that idea and everything documents written for the original already rely on (`#title`, `#subtitle`, `#post`, `#p`, the comma separated `#list`, code blocks
labelled with the name of the bag around them and the `<<<name>>>` links between them), and adds what a blog
needs around it.

This document is itself a literate program #cite{knuth1984lp}: the explanation and the source are the same
text. The code appears in the order that is easiest to follow, and the Go files are stitched together from
named chunks in the Assembly section at the end.

#h2{What you get}

- Posts and pages written in brashtag, with metadata in ordinary bags such as `#title`, `#date` and `#tags`.
- Automatic paragraphs, so a post reads like prose instead of a forest of `#p` bags.
- Headings with anchors and an optional table of contents, lists, tables, sidenotes and margin notes, call-out boxes,
  collapsible details, figures, video, embedded YouTube, abbreviations, keyboard keys and raw HTML.
- Syntax highlighting for Go, Python, JavaScript, the C family, shell, SQL, JSON, YAML, CSS and brashtag
  itself, written from scratch, plus copy buttons on code blocks.
- Literate posts: a bag that holds code becomes a labelled chunk, and `<<<chunk>>>` lines link to it.
- A default theme in the manner of Edward Tufte's books: a narrow column of text with notes, captions and
  dates in a wide margin.
- Math through MathJax and diagrams through Mermaid, loaded only on pages that use them.
- A paginated home page, tag pages, a tag index, a yearly archive, previous and next links, related posts,
  reading time, excerpts with an explicit `#more` break, drafts and scheduled posts.
- Atom and RSS feeds, a sitemap, `robots.txt`, a 404 page, Open Graph tags and canonical links.
- Client-side full text search over a JSON index, with no server.
- A light and a dark theme that follow the reader's system setting and can be toggled.
- Page bundles: a post kept as `posts/name/index.bt` can carry its own images.
- A site that works under a sub path such as `https://user.github.io/blog/`.
- A checker that reports every broken internal link and fragment after the build.
- `barkdown serve`, a development server that rebuilds on change and reloads the browser.
- `barkdown init`, `barkdown new` and `barkdown render`, for starting a site, a post, or converting one file.

#h2{A site on disk}

A barkdown site is a directory like this one. Only `site.bt` and `posts` are needed; everything else is optional.

#site layout{
  The layout is shown here for reference; it is not part of any generated file.

  ```
  site.bt                    site configuration, in brashtag
  posts/2026-11-01-hello.bt  a post; the date prefix becomes its date
  posts/trip/index.bt        a page bundle; other files in posts/trip/ are copied next to it
  pages/about.bt             a page, published at /about/
  static/                    copied to the output unchanged
  templates/                 optional overrides for the built-in templates and assets
  public/                    the generated site (created by barkdown build)
  ```
}

The commands are:

#usage text{
  ```
  barkdown init [dir]                  create a new site with example content
  barkdown new post|page "Title"       start a new post (as a draft) or page
  barkdown build [flags]               build the site into public/
  barkdown serve [flags]               build, serve and rebuild on change
  barkdown render [file.bt]            convert one document to a standalone HTML page
  barkdown version
  ```
}

#h2{Plan of the program}

The program has a short pipeline. A document's text is parsed into a brashtag tree; its metadata bags are
taken off the top; the rest is rendered to HTML. The site loads its configuration and every document, works out
URLs, tags, archives and links between posts, and then writes HTML through Go's `html/template`, followed by
the feeds, the sitemap and the search index. The sections below follow that order: the parser, documents,
rendering, highlighting, the site, the output, the theme, the server, the command line, an example site and
the tests.
#h1{The notation and its parser}

Brashtag describes a tree with three kinds of node. A bag is written `#tag{ ... }` and holds other nodes;
a code is a run of backticks, any text, and the same run again; a blob is plain text up to the next `#`,
backtick or closing brace. Those three characters, and an opening brace outside a bag's tag, are the only
special ones. Anything that contains them goes between backticks, with more backticks around it than it holds
in a row.

Barkdown uses the parser from the brashtag repository, `parser.go`, copied byte for byte into the package
`barkdown/brashtag` (MIT licence, copyright 2021 Pratik Deoghare). Copying it, rather than importing the module,
keeps barkdown free of the dependencies that the repository's other commands need, such as Graphviz bindings.
It is shown here so that the reader knows exactly what the rest of the program can rely on.

#h2{Nodes}

#parser header{
  ```
  // Package brashtag provides parser for parsing
  // files written in brashtag notation.
  package brashtag

  import (
  	"fmt"
  	"strings"
  )
  ```
}

#parser nodes{
  Every node is a `fmt.Stringer`, and printing a tree gives back the text it was parsed from. The unexported
  method `isNode` closes the interface, so the only nodes are the three types below. Each type is a struct
  wrapping a pointer, which lets the setters change a node that has been copied by value.

  ```
  type Node interface {
  	fmt.Stringer
  	isNode()
  }

  type bag struct {
  	tag  string
  	kids []Node
  }

  // Bag has a tag and children.
  type Bag struct {
  	*bag
  }

  var _ Node = Bag{}

  func (Bag) isNode() {}

  func NewBag(tag string, kids ...Node) Bag {
  	return Bag{
  		&bag{
  			tag:  tag,
  			kids: kids,
  		},
  	}
  }

  func (b Bag) AddKids(kids ...Node) {
  	b.kids = append(b.kids, kids...)
  }

  func (b Bag) RemoveKid(i int) {
  	if i < len(b.kids) {
  		b.kids = append(b.kids[:i], b.kids[i+1:]...)
  	}
  }

  func (b Bag) SetTag(tag string) {
  	b.tag = tag
  }

  func (b Bag) Kids() []Node {
  	return b.kids
  }

  func (b Bag) Tag() string {
  	return b.tag
  }

  func (b Bag) String() string {
  	var text []string

  	for _, kid := range b.Kids() {
  		text = append(text, kid.String())
  	}

  	return fmt.Sprintf("#%s{%s}", b.tag, strings.Join(text, ""))
  }
  ```

  #parser code nodes{
    A code node remembers its backtick run as its tag. Barkdown uses the length of that run to tell inline code
    (one or two backticks) from a code block (three or more), the same rule as Markdown.

    ```
    type code struct {
    	tag  string
    	text string
    }

    type Code struct {
    	*code
    }

    var _ Node = Code{}

    func (Code) isNode() {}

    func NewCode(tag, text string) Code {
    	return Code{
    		&code{
    			tag:  tag,
    			text: text,
    		},
    	}
    }

    func (c Code) SetTag(tag string) {
    	c.tag = tag
    }

    func (c Code) SetText(text string) {
    	c.text = text
    }

    func (c Code) Text() string {
    	return c.text
    }

    func (c Code) Tag() string {
    	return c.tag
    }

    func (c Code) String() string {
    	return fmt.Sprintf("%s%s%s", c.tag, c.text, c.tag)
    }
    ```
  }

  #parser blob nodes{
    ```
    type blob struct {
    	text string
    }

    type Blob struct {
    	*blob
    }

    var _ Node = Blob{}

    func (Blob) isNode() {}

    func NewBlob(text string) Blob {
    	return Blob{
    		&blob{text: text},
    	}
    }

    func (b Blob) SetText(text string) {
    	b.text = text
    }

    func (b Blob) Text() string {
    	return b.text
    }

    func (b Blob) String() string {
    	return fmt.Sprint(b.text)
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Parsing}

#parser entry{
  `Parse` reads exactly one bag. Text after it is an error, and the error message carries up to a hundred
  characters of the unread rest. Two consequences matter later. First, a document is a sequence of nodes, not
  one bag, so barkdown wraps it as `#{ ... }` the way the original barkdown does. Second, when a stray closing brace ends the
  wrapper early, `parseBag` returns no error but leaves text unread; `Parse` then wraps a nil error, and the
  message starts with the odd text `%!w(<nil>)`. Barkdown recognises both shapes when it reports errors with
  line numbers.

  ```
  // Parse parses the text and returns its brashtag tree.
  func Parse(text string) (Node, error) {
  	root, rem, err := parseBag([]byte(text))

  	if len(rem) != 0 {
  		n := 100
  		if len(rem) < 100 {
  			n = len(rem)
  		}
  		msg := rem[:n]
  		return nil, fmt.Errorf("%w: %s", err, string(msg))
  	}
  	if err != nil {
  		return nil, err
  	}
  	return root, nil
  }
  ```
}

#parser bags{
  A bag's tag is everything from after the `#` up to the first opening brace, so a stray `#` in prose silently
  swallows the text up to the next brace into a tag. After the brace the parser dispatches on the next byte
  until it meets the closing brace.

  ```
  func parseBag(text []byte) (Node, []byte, error) {
  	root := NewBag("")

  	c := 0
  	bagOpen := false
  	for c < len(text) {
  		if text[c] != '{' {
  			c++
  		} else {
  			bagOpen = true
  			break
  		}
  	}

  	if !bagOpen && c == len(text) {
  		return nil, text, fmt.Errorf("unopened bag missing { after #")
  	}

  	root.SetTag(string(text[1:c]))
  	text = text[c+1:]

  	var k Node
  	var err error

  	for len(text) > 0 {
  		switch {
  		case text[0] == '#':
  			k, text, err = parseBag(text)
  			if err != nil {
  				return nil, text, err
  			}
  			root.AddKids(k)

  		case text[0] == '`':
  			k, text, err = parseCode(text)
  			if err != nil {
  				return nil, text, err
  			}
  			root.AddKids(k)

  		case text[0] == '}':
  			return root, text[1:], nil

  		default:
  			k, text, err = parseBlob(text)
  			if err != nil {
  				return nil, text, err
  			}
  			root.AddKids(k)
  		}
  	}

  	return nil, text, fmt.Errorf("bag not closed")
  }
  ```
}

#parser code{
  A code node ends at the first later run of the same number of backticks. The loop bound `len(text)-c`
  means a closing run must be followed by at least one more byte; inside barkdown's `#{ ... }` wrapper the final
  brace always provides it.

  ```
  func parseCode(text []byte) (Node, []byte, error) {
  	root := NewCode("", "")
  	c := 0
  	for c < len(text) {
  		if text[c] == '`' {
  			c++
  		} else {
  			break
  		}
  	}
  	root.SetTag(string(text[0:c]))
  	j := c
  	for j < len(text)-c {
  		if string(text[j:j+c]) != root.tag {
  			j++
  		} else {
  			root.SetText(string(text[c:j]))
  			return root, text[j+c:], nil
  		}
  	}

  	return nil, text, fmt.Errorf("code not closed")
  }
  ```
}

#parser blobs{
  A blob runs to the next special character. An opening brace in a blob is an error, because it can only be a
  mistake: a tag needs a `#` before it. So a post that shows JSON or Go must put it between backticks.

  ```
  func parseBlob(text []byte) (Node, []byte, error) {
  	root := NewBlob("")
  	j := 0

  loop:
  	for j < len(text) {
  		switch text[j] {
  		case '`', '#', '}':
  			break loop
  		case '{':
  			return nil, text, fmt.Errorf("unexpected {: %s", neighbourhood(text, j, 100))
  		default:
  			j++
  		}
  	}
  	root.SetText(string(text[:j]))

  	return root, text[j:], nil
  }

  func neighbourhood(text []byte, loc, size int) []byte {
  	l := loc - size
  	if l < 0 {
  		l = 0
  	}
  	r := loc + size
  	if r > len(text) {
  		r = len(text)
  	}
  	return text[l:r]
  }
  ```
}
#h1{Documents}

A document is one `.bt` file: a post or a page. This section turns its text into a `Doc`, which holds the
metadata the templates need and the body that the renderer will turn into HTML.

#h2{Parsing a whole file, with line numbers}

#parse a document{
  The parser reads one bag, so the text is wrapped in an anonymous bag `#{ ... }`, exactly as the original barkdown does.
  Leading and trailing white space is trimmed first so that the wrapper hugs the content. Windows line endings
  are normalised, because the code blocks of a post should not carry carriage returns into the HTML.

  ```
  // parseBrashtag parses a whole document. The text is wrapped in an anonymous
  // bag, as the original barkdown does, because the parser reads exactly one bag.
  func parseBrashtag(src string) (bt.Bag, error) {
  	src = strings.ReplaceAll(src, "\r\n", "\n")
  	body := strings.TrimSpace(src)
  	wrapped := "#{" + body + "}"
  	tree, err := bt.Parse(wrapped)
  	if err != nil {
  		lead := len(src) - len(strings.TrimLeftFunc(src, unicode.IsSpace))
  		return bt.Bag{}, locate(src, wrapped, lead, err)
  	}
  	return tree.(bt.Bag), nil
  }
  ```

  #parse error type{
    The parser's messages say what went wrong but not where. A writer with a long post needs a line number,
    so errors are reported as a `ParseError`.

    ```
    // ParseError is a syntax error in a document, with the line it was found on
    // (0 when the parser gives no clue).
    type ParseError struct {
    	Line int
    	Msg  string
    }

    func (e *ParseError) Error() string {
    	if e.Line > 0 {
    		return fmt.Sprintf("line %d: %s", e.Line, e.Msg)
    	}
    	return e.Msg
    }
    ```
  }

  #locate a parse error{
    Most of the parser's errors end with `": "` followed by the unread rest of the input, as the parser
    section showed. That rest is a suffix of the wrapped text, so searching for it finds the offset where the
    parser stopped. The message itself may contain `": "` too, so every split point is tried from the left and
    the first one whose tail occurs in the text wins: that is the longest tail, the most specific match.

    The offset is then translated back to the original file: subtract the two bytes of `#{` and add the white
    space that was trimmed from the front. For an unexpected opening brace the parser reports the start of the
    blob, so the position is moved forward to the brace itself. The messages are rewritten to say how to fix
    the problem, which is nearly always the same: put the character between backticks.

    ```
    func locate(src, wrapped string, lead int, err error) error {
    	msg := err.Error()
    	head, off := msg, -1
    	for i := 0; i+2 < len(msg); i++ {
    		if msg[i:i+2] != ": " {
    			continue
    		}
    		if k := strings.Index(wrapped, msg[i+2:]); k >= 0 {
    			head, off = msg[:i], k
    			break
    		}
    	}
    	var text string
    	switch {
    	case strings.HasPrefix(head, "%!w(<nil>)"):
    		text = "unmatched '}' ends the document early; write it inside backticks"
    	case strings.HasPrefix(head, "unexpected {"):
    		text = "'{' outside a tag; write it inside backticks"
    		if off >= 0 {
    			if j := strings.IndexByte(wrapped[off:], '{'); j >= 0 {
    				off += j
    			}
    		}
    	case strings.HasPrefix(head, "unopened bag"):
    		text = "'#' starts a tag but no '{' follows; write it inside backticks"
    	case strings.HasPrefix(head, "code not closed"):
    		text = "code is not closed: the backticks that open it never appear again"
    	case strings.HasPrefix(head, "bag not closed"):
    		text = "a #tag{ is never closed: a '}' is missing"
    	default:
    		text = head
    	}
    	line := 0
    	if off >= 0 {
    		pos := min(max(lead+off-2, 0), len(src))
    		line = 1 + strings.Count(src[:pos], "\n")
    	}
    	return &ParseError{Line: line, Msg: text}
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Text without markup}

#text helpers{
  Several places need the words of a node without its markup: metadata values, image captions used as `alt`
  text, heading slugs, search. `textOf` keeps the text of blobs and code and drops the tags.

  ```
  // textOf returns the text of a node with all tags removed.
  func textOf(n bt.Node) string {
  	switch x := n.(type) {
  	case bt.Blob:
  		return x.Text()
  	case bt.Code:
  		return x.Text()
  	case bt.Bag:
  		return textOfNodes(x.Kids())
  	}
  	return ""
  }

  func textOfNodes(nodes []bt.Node) string {
  	var b strings.Builder
  	for _, n := range nodes {
  		b.WriteString(textOf(n))
  	}
  	return b.String()
  }
  ```

  #plain text of a body{
    For the search index and word counts the text must also read correctly. Comments and raw HTML are left
    out. Block elements and code blocks are surrounded by spaces so that the last word of one list item does
    not run into the first word of the next, while inline bags are not, so `#b{bold}er` stays one word.
    The target of a link, image or reference is not text a reader sees, so it is skipped as well, and so is the
    timestamp at the start of a thought, which the page shows formatted in the margin rather than as words. Finally white
    space is collapsed.

    ```
    // plainText is the readable text of a document body, for search, word
    // counts and summaries.
    func plainText(nodes []bt.Node) string {
    	var b strings.Builder
    	var walk func([]bt.Node)
    	walk = func(nodes []bt.Node) {
    		for _, n := range nodes {
    			switch x := n.(type) {
    			case bt.Blob:
    				b.WriteString(x.Text())
    			case bt.Code:
    				if isFence(x) {
    					b.WriteString(" " + x.Text() + " ")
    				} else {
    					b.WriteString(x.Text())
    				}
    			case bt.Bag:
    				tag := strings.TrimSpace(x.Tag())
    				if tag == "comment" || tag == "html" {
    					continue
    				}
    				h, known := handlers[tag]
    				space := !known || h.block
    				if space {
    					b.WriteString(" ")
    				}
    				kids := x.Kids()
    				if targetTags[tag] {
    					_, kids = firstWord(kids)
    				}
    				if isThought(x) {
    					_, kids = firstWord(kids)
    					if clock, after := firstWord(kids); clockWord.MatchString(clock) {
    						kids = after
    					}
    				}
    				walk(kids)
    				if space {
    					b.WriteString(" ")
    				}
    			}
    		}
    	}
    	walk(nodes)
    	return strings.Join(strings.Fields(b.String()), " ")
    }

    var targetTags = map[string]bool{"link": true, "a": true, "ref": true, "img": true, "image": true,
    	"figure": true, "video": true, "audio": true, "youtube": true, "abbr": true}

    // truncateWords keeps the first n words of s.
    func truncateWords(s string, n int) string {
    	words := strings.Fields(s)
    	if len(words) <= n {
    		return strings.Join(words, " ")
    	}
    	return strings.Join(words[:n], " ") + "…"
    }
    ```
  }

  #slugify{
    Slugs name files, tags and heading anchors. Letters and digits of any script are kept, in lower case;
    every other run of characters becomes one hyphen, and no hyphen is left at either end. Keeping non-Latin
    letters means a post titled in Marathi gets a readable URL instead of an empty one. Combining marks must be
    kept as well: in Devanagari the vowel signs and the virama are marks, not letters, and dropping them would
    cut `नमस्ते` into pieces.

    ```
    // slugify turns a title into a URL-friendly name: "Hello, World!" -> "hello-world".
    func slugify(s string) string {
    	var b strings.Builder
    	dash := false
    	for _, r := range strings.ToLower(s) {
    		if unicode.IsLetter(r) || unicode.IsDigit(r) || (unicode.IsMark(r) && b.Len() > 0) {
    			if dash && b.Len() > 0 {
    				b.WriteByte('-')
    			}
    			dash = false
    			b.WriteRune(r)
    		} else {
    			dash = true
    		}
    	}
    	return b.String()
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Metadata}

Metadata are ordinary bags at the top level of a document, so a post starts like this:

#sample front matter{
  ```
  #title{Hello, barkdown}
  #date{2026-11-01}
  #tags{go, brashtag}
  #summary{What this blog is about.}
  ```
}

The same bags may instead be grouped inside `#meta{ ... }`. A bag that is not metadata is part of the body. This
keeps documents written for the original barkdown working: their `#title` and `#subtitle` simply become the post's title and subtitle,
which the template displays.

#doc type{
  The `Doc` holds the metadata as typed fields, the remaining body, and a group of fields that the site fills in
  while building, such as the URL, the rendered HTML and the neighbouring posts. Every metadata bag is also kept
  in `Meta` as text, so a custom template can use a tag barkdown does not know, say `#mood{cheerful}`, as
  `.Doc.Meta.mood`. Unknown tags only count as metadata inside `#meta`; at the top level they are content.

  ```
  // Latest returns the most recent thought of a post, or nil.
  func (d *Doc) Latest() *Thought {
  	var latest *Thought
  	for _, t := range d.Thoughts {
  		if latest == nil || t.Time.After(latest.Time) {
  			latest = t
  		}
  	}
  	return latest
  }

  // Doc is one post or page.
  type Doc struct {
  	Source   string // path of the file, relative to the site root
  	Kind     string // "post" or "page"
  	Title    string
  	Subtitle string
  	Author   string
  	Summary  string // from #summary{} or #description{}, as plain text
  	Date     time.Time
  	Updated  time.Time
  	Tags     []string
  	Draft    bool
  	Slug     string
  	Layout   string            // template to use instead of the default
  	Weight   int               // order of pages in the menu
  	Meta     map[string]string // every metadata bag, by tag, as text

  	body   []bt.Node // the document without its metadata
  	bundle string    // directory of a page bundle, "" otherwise

  	// Filled in by the site while building.
  	URL         string
  	Content     template.HTML
  	Excerpt     template.HTML
  	HasMore     bool
  	Description string
  	Text        string
  	Words       int
  	ReadingTime int
  	Math        bool
  	Mermaid     bool
  	TagRefs     []*Tag
  	Prev, Next  *Doc
  	Related     []*Doc

  	Thoughts    []*Thought
  	LastThought time.Time

  	excerptMath, excerptMermaid bool
  }

  var metaTags = map[string]bool{
  	"title": true, "subtitle": true, "author": true, "summary": true,
  	"description": true, "date": true, "updated": true, "tags": true,
  	"draft": true, "slug": true, "layout": true, "weight": true, "order": true,
  }
  ```
}

#parse doc{
  `ParseDoc` walks the top level once and sorts each node into metadata or body. Errors carry the file name and,
  when known, the line in the `file:line:` form that editors and terminals turn into links.

  ```
  // fileError prefixes an error with its file and, for a ParseError, its line.
  func fileError(source string, err error) error {
  	var pe *ParseError
  	if errors.As(err, &pe) && pe.Line > 0 {
  		return fmt.Errorf("%s:%d: %s", source, pe.Line, pe.Msg)
  	}
  	return fmt.Errorf("%s: %v", source, err)
  }

  // ParseDoc parses a document and separates its metadata from its body.
  func ParseDoc(source, text string) (*Doc, error) {
  	tree, err := parseBrashtag(text)
  	if err != nil {
  		return nil, fileError(source, err)
  	}
  	d := &Doc{Source: source, Meta: map[string]string{}}
  	for _, kid := range tree.Kids() {
  		b, ok := kid.(bt.Bag)
  		tag := ""
  		if ok {
  			tag = strings.TrimSpace(b.Tag())
  		}
  		switch {
  		case tag == "meta":
  			for _, m := range b.Kids() {
  				if mb, ok := m.(bt.Bag); ok {
  					if err := d.setMeta(mb); err != nil {
  						return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: %v", source, err)
  					}
  				}
  			}
  		case metaTags[tag]:
  			if err := d.setMeta(b); err != nil {
  				return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: %v", source, err)
  			}
  		default:
  			d.body = append(d.body, kid)
  		}
  	}
  	return d, nil
  }
  ```
}

#set a metadata field{
  A value is the bag's text with white space collapsed, so a title may be wrapped over two lines. Empty bags are
  ignored, except `#draft{}`, whose presence is the whole point: it marks a draft unless it says `false` or `no`.

  ```
  func (d *Doc) setMeta(b bt.Bag) error {
  	tag := strings.TrimSpace(b.Tag())
  	val := strings.Join(strings.Fields(textOf(b)), " ")
  	d.Meta[tag] = val
  	if val == "" && tag != "draft" {
  		return nil
  	}
  	var err error
  	switch tag {
  	case "title":
  		d.Title = val
  	case "subtitle":
  		d.Subtitle = val
  	case "author":
  		d.Author = val
  	case "summary", "description":
  		d.Summary = val
  	case "date":
  		d.Date, err = parseDate(val)
  	case "updated":
  		d.Updated, err = parseDate(val)
  	case "tags":
  		d.Tags = splitList(val)
  	case "draft":
  		d.Draft = val != "false" && val != "no"
  	case "slug":
  		d.Slug = slugify(val)
  	case "layout":
  		d.Layout = val
  	case "weight":
  		d.Weight, err = strconv.Atoi(val)
  	case "order":
  		if val != "oldest" && val != "newest" {
  			err = fmt.Errorf("the order of thoughts is oldest or newest")
  		}
  	}
  	if err != nil {
  		return fmt.Errorf("#%s{%s}: %v", tag, val, err)
  	}
  	return nil
  }
  ```

  #dates and lists{
    Dates are read in the writer's local time zone. A handful of layouts are accepted, from a bare day to
    RFC 3339; anything else is an error rather than a silently missing date. Tag lists are comma separated,
    like the original barkdown's `#list`, and duplicates are dropped.

    ```
    var dateLayouts = []string{
    	"2006-01-02", "2006-01-02 15:04", "2006-01-02 15:04:05", "2006-01-02T15:04",
    	time.RFC3339, "2 January 2006", "January 2, 2006", "2 Jan 2006",
    }

    func parseDate(s string) (time.Time, error) {
    	for _, layout := range dateLayouts {
    		if t, err := time.ParseInLocation(layout, s, time.Local); err == nil {
    			return t, nil
    		}
    	}
    	return time.Time{}, fmt.Errorf("unrecognised date; use YYYY-MM-DD or YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM")
    }

    func splitList(s string) []string {
    	var out []string
    	seen := map[string]bool{}
    	for _, part := range strings.Split(s, ",") {
    		part = strings.TrimSpace(part)
    		if part != "" && !seen[part] {
    			seen[part] = true
    			out = append(out, part)
    		}
    	}
    	return out
    }
    ```
  }
}

#names from files{
  A file name says a lot already. `2026-11-01-hello-world.bt` names its date and its slug, a convention shared
  with Jekyll and Hugo, so neither needs to be repeated inside. For a page bundle, `posts/trip/index.bt`, the
  directory is the name. Metadata in the file wins over the name; the site applies that rule.

  ```
  var datePrefix = regexp.MustCompile(`^(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})-(.+)$`)

  // nameParts derives a slug, and a date if the name starts with one, from a
  // file name such as "2026-11-01-hello-world.bt" or a bundle's directory.
  func nameParts(path string) (slug string, date time.Time) {
  	name := strings.TrimSuffix(filepath.Base(path), ".bt")
  	if name == "index" {
  		name = filepath.Base(filepath.Dir(path))
  	}
  	if m := datePrefix.FindStringSubmatch(name); m != nil {
  		if t, err := time.ParseInLocation("2006-01-02", m[1], time.Local); err == nil {
  			date, name = t, m[2]
  		}
  	}
  	return slugify(name), date
  }

  // humanize makes a title out of a slug, for documents that lack #title{}.
  func humanize(slug string) string {
  	s := strings.ReplaceAll(slug, "-", " ")
  	for i, r := range s {
  		return string(unicode.ToUpper(r)) + s[i+len(string(r)):]
  	}
  	return s
  }
  ```
}
#h1{Rendering}

The original barkdown's renderer is one recursive function: a blob becomes its text, inline code becomes
`<code>`, a code block becomes `<pre>`, and a bag becomes an element named after its tag. This renderer has the
same shape with four changes. Text is HTML-escaped, so a post about generics can say `a < b` safely. Paragraphs are found
automatically from blank lines. Tags are looked up in a table of handlers, each of which knows whether it makes
a block or an inline element. And the renderer keeps some state while it walks the tree.

#h2{The renderer and its state}

#renderer type{
  A renderer is used for one document. On its way it collects the headings for a table of contents, the
  number of sidenotes and margin notes, whether the page needs MathJax or Mermaid, and warnings about tags it did not understand or links
  it could not resolve. `chunks` holds the names of literate chunks, explained near the end of this section, and
  `ids` makes every `id` attribute in the page unique.

  ```
  // Renderer turns the body of one document into HTML.
  type Renderer struct {
  	site     *Site // for links between documents and the base path; may be nil
  	excerpt  bool  // rendering an excerpt: no footnotes, no table of contents
  	chunks   map[string]bool
  	chunk    string // the literate chunk being rendered, if any
  	ids      map[string]int
  	headings []heading
  	notes    int // sidenotes so far
  	thoughts []*Thought
  	newest   bool // show timelines newest first
  	margins  int  // margin notes so far
  	math     bool
  	mermaid  bool
  	Warnings []string
  }

  type heading struct {
  	level    int
  	id, text string
  }

  func NewRenderer(site *Site) *Renderer {
  	return &Renderer{site: site, chunks: map[string]bool{}, ids: map[string]int{}}
  }

  func (r *Renderer) warnf(format string, args ...any) {
  	r.Warnings = append(r.Warnings, fmt.Sprintf(format, args...))
  }

  func (r *Renderer) uniqueID(base string) string {
  	r.ids[base]++
  	if n := r.ids[base]; n > 1 {
  		return fmt.Sprintf("%s-%d", base, n)
  	}
  	return base
  }
  ```
}

#render a body{
  Rendering a body has three steps. Chunks are found first, because a reference may appear before the chunk it
  names. Then the nodes are rendered as a flow. The table of contents can only be
  built when all headings have been seen, so `#toc{}` first leaves a marker made of private-use characters,
  which cannot occur in escaped text, and the marker is replaced at the end.

  ```
  const tocMark = "toc"

  // Render renders the body of a document.
  func (r *Renderer) Render(nodes []bt.Node) string {
  	r.findChunks(nodes)
  	out := r.flow(nodes)
  	if strings.Contains(out, tocMark) {
  		out = strings.ReplaceAll(out, tocMark, r.toc())
  	}
  	return out
  }
  ```
}

#h2{Flow and paragraphs}

#flow{
  A flow is a sequence of nodes in which blank lines separate paragraphs: the body of a post, a block
  quote, a call-out, a list item. Inline pieces (text, inline code, inline bags such as `#b`) accumulate in the
  current paragraph. A blank line inside a blob, a code block, or a block bag such as a table closes it. A
  paragraph is written as `<p>` only if it holds something besides white space, so the spaces and newlines
  between two block bags do not turn into empty paragraphs.

  This is what lets a post be written as plain prose. Documents written for the original barkdown that wrap
  every paragraph in `#p` still work: `#p` is a block handler, and the white space around it produces nothing.

  One construct needs to see its neighbours: a run of thoughts, which becomes one timeline (described in its
  own section below). So the loop runs over indices, and when it meets a thought it hands the whole run to
  `timeline` and continues after it.

  ```
  var blankLine = regexp.MustCompile(`\n[ \t]*\n`)

  // flow renders nodes in which blank lines separate paragraphs.
  func (r *Renderer) flow(nodes []bt.Node) string {
  	var out, para strings.Builder
  	flush := func() {
  		if s := strings.TrimSpace(para.String()); s != "" {
  			out.WriteString("<p>" + s + "</p>\n")
  		}
  		para.Reset()
  	}
  	for i := 0; i < len(nodes); i++ {
  		switch x := nodes[i].(type) {
  		case bt.Blob:
  			for k, part := range blankLine.Split(x.Text(), -1) {
  				if k > 0 {
  					flush()
  				}
  				para.WriteString(html.EscapeString(part))
  			}
  		case bt.Code:
  			if isFence(x) {
  				flush()
  				out.WriteString(r.fence(x) + "\n")
  			} else {
  				para.WriteString(r.inlineCode(x))
  			}
  		case bt.Bag:
  			if isThought(x) {
  				end := thoughtRun(nodes, i)
  				flush()
  				out.WriteString(r.timeline(nodes[i:end]) + "\n")
  				i = end - 1
  				continue
  			}
  			h, block := r.bag(x)
  			if block {
  				flush()
  				if h != "" {
  					out.WriteString(h + "\n")
  				}
  			} else {
  				para.WriteString(h)
  			}
  		}
  	}
  	flush()
  	return out.String()
  }
  ```

  #inline content{
    Inside a heading, a link or a table cell there are no paragraphs; the content is rendered as one run.
    `compactFlow` is for list items, which may hold several paragraphs but usually hold one; a
    single paragraph loses its `<p>` so that short items are not spaced like prose.

    ```
    // inline renders nodes as one run of text, without paragraphs.
    func (r *Renderer) inline(nodes []bt.Node) string {
    	var b strings.Builder
    	for _, n := range nodes {
    		switch x := n.(type) {
    		case bt.Blob:
    			b.WriteString(html.EscapeString(x.Text()))
    		case bt.Code:
    			if isFence(x) {
    				b.WriteString(r.fence(x))
    			} else {
    				b.WriteString(r.inlineCode(x))
    			}
    		case bt.Bag:
    			h, _ := r.bag(x)
    			b.WriteString(h)
    		}
    	}
    	return strings.TrimSpace(b.String())
    }

    func (r *Renderer) compactFlow(nodes []bt.Node) string {
    	out := strings.TrimSpace(r.flow(nodes))
    	if strings.HasPrefix(out, "<p>") && strings.HasSuffix(out, "</p>") && strings.Count(out, "<p>") == 1 {
    		return out[3 : len(out)-4]
    	}
    	return out
    }
    ```
  }

  #inline code{
    Inline code is escaped and wrapped in `<code>`. One special case comes from the literate tools: a span
    with a single backtick whose text starts and ends with a dollar sign is LaTeX math, set inline by MathJax.

    ```
    func (r *Renderer) inlineCode(c bt.Code) string {
    	t := c.Text()
    	if len(c.Tag()) == 1 && len(t) >= 2 && t[0] == '$' && t[len(t)-1] == '$' {
    		r.math = true
    		return `<span class="math">\(` + html.EscapeString(t[1:len(t)-1]) + `\)</span>`
    	}
    	return "<code>" + html.EscapeString(t) + "</code>"
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{The table of tags}

#tag dispatch{
  Each tag maps to a handler: a function from the bag to HTML, and a flag saying whether the result is a block.
  The table is filled in `init` rather than in a composite literal because the handlers call `r.bag`, which
  reads the table; Go rejects that cycle in a package-level initialiser. A tag is trimmed before the lookup, so
  `#b {x}` and `#b{x}` mean the same.

  ```
  type handler struct {
  	block bool
  	fn    func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string
  }

  var handlers map[string]handler

  // bag renders one bag and says whether the result is a block element.
  func (r *Renderer) bag(b bt.Bag) (string, bool) {
  	tag := strings.TrimSpace(b.Tag())
  	if h, ok := handlers[tag]; ok {
  		return h.fn(r, b), h.block
  	}
  	return r.unknown(b)
  }

  func init() {
  	handlers = map[string]handler{}
  	<<<register simple tags>>>
  	<<<register structural tags>>>
  	<<<register links and media>>>
  	<<<register notes and asides>>>
  	<<<register code, math and diagrams>>>
  }
  ```
}

#register simple tags{
  Most inline tags are HTML elements of the same name: `#b{bold}`, `#em{stress}`, `#kbd{Ctrl}`, `#sup{2}`.
  Block containers such as `#blockquote` and `#section` hold a flow, so they may contain paragraphs. The original barkdown's
  `#post` becomes a `div` with class `post`, its `#title` a top-level heading and `#subtitle` a subtitle (at the
  top of a document those two are metadata instead, as described earlier). `#comment` vanishes, which is how the
  brashtag README annotates its own examples.

  ```
  for _, tag := range []string{"b", "i", "em", "strong", "u", "s", "del", "ins", "sup", "sub",
  	"small", "mark", "kbd", "q", "cite", "span", "var", "samp", "dfn"} {
  	el := tag
  	handlers[tag] = handler{false, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  		return "<" + el + ">" + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</" + el + ">"
  	}}
  }
  container := func(tag, el, class string) {
  	open := "<" + el + ">"
  	if class != "" {
  		open = "<" + el + ` class="` + class + `">`
  	}
  	handlers[tag] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  		return open + "\n" + r.flow(b.Kids()) + "</" + el + ">"
  	}}
  }
  container("post", "div", "post")
  container("div", "div", "")
  container("center", "div", "center")
  container("section", "section", "")
  container("article", "article", "")
  container("aside", "aside", "")
  container("header", "header", "")
  container("footer", "footer", "")
  container("blockquote", "blockquote", "")
  container("quote", "blockquote", "")
  container("li", "li", "")
  container("dl", "dl", "")
  container("dd", "dd", "")
  handlers["dt"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return "<dt>" + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</dt>" }}
  handlers["p"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return "<p>" + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</p>" }}
  handlers["title"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return "<h1>" + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</h1>" }}
  handlers["subtitle"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  	return `<p class="subtitle">` + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</p>"
  }}
  handlers["by"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  	return `<footer class="by">— ` + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</footer>"
  }}
  handlers["br"] = handler{false, func(*Renderer, bt.Bag) string { return "<br>" }}
  handlers["hr"] = handler{true, func(*Renderer, bt.Bag) string { return "<hr>" }}
  handlers["comment"] = handler{false, func(*Renderer, bt.Bag) string { return "" }}
  handlers["more"] = handler{true, func(*Renderer, bt.Bag) string { return "" }}
  ```
}

#h2{Headings and the table of contents}

#register structural tags{
  Headings, lists, tables and the table of contents are the structural tags.

  ```
  for level := 1; level <= 6; level++ {
  	handlers[fmt.Sprintf("h%d", level)] = headingHandler(level)
  }
  handlers["toc"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  	if r.excerpt {
  		return ""
  	}
  	return tocMark
  }}
  handlers["list"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return r.list(b, "ul", ",") }}
  handlers["ul"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return r.list(b, "ul", "\n") }}
  handlers["ol"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return r.list(b, "ol", "\n") }}
  handlers["table"] = handler{true, (*Renderer).table}
  handlers["tr"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return "<tr>" + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</tr>" }}
  handlers["td"] = handler{false, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return "<td>" + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</td>" }}
  handlers["th"] = handler{false, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return "<th>" + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</th>" }}
  ```
}

#headings{
  A heading gets an `id` made from its text, so that sections can be linked to, and a small `#` anchor that the
  stylesheet shows on hover. Headings of levels two to four are remembered for the table of contents, with their
  plain text: the heading's own HTML might contain a link, and links may not nest.

  ```
  func headingHandler(level int) handler {
  	return handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  		inner := r.inline(b.Kids())
  		text := strings.Join(strings.Fields(textOf(b)), " ")
  		base := slugify(text)
  		if base == "" {
  			base = "section"
  		}
  		id := r.uniqueID(base)
  		if level >= 2 && level <= 4 {
  			r.headings = append(r.headings, heading{level, id, html.EscapeString(text)})
  		}
  		return fmt.Sprintf(`<h%d id="%s">%s <a class="anchor" href="#%s" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h%d>`,
  			level, id, inner, id, level)
  	}}
  }
  ```

  #table of contents{
    The headings form a nested list. `open` counts the lists currently open; their depth follows the heading
    levels relative to the shallowest one. Going deeper opens lists, coming back closes them, and a heading at
    the same depth closes the previous item and opens the next. Every `ul` is opened together with an `li`, so
    the result is valid HTML even when a document jumps from level two straight to level four.

    The contents get an anchor, and right after them comes a link back to it, which the theme pins to the
    corner of the window: in a long post a reader can always return to the contents and jump elsewhere. It is
    emitted here rather than in a template because only the renderer knows whether a post has contents.

    ```
    func (r *Renderer) toc() string {
    	if len(r.headings) == 0 {
    		return ""
    	}
    	top := 6
    	for _, h := range r.headings {
    		top = min(top, h.level)
    	}
    	var b strings.Builder
    	id := r.uniqueID("contents")
    	fmt.Fprintf(&b, `<nav class="toc" id="%s" aria-label="In this post"><p class="toc-title">In this post</p>`, id)
    	open := 0
    	for i, h := range r.headings {
    		depth := h.level - top + 1
    		switch {
    		case i == 0 || depth > open:
    			for open < depth {
    				b.WriteString("<ul><li>")
    				open++
    			}
    		default:
    			for open > depth {
    				b.WriteString("</li></ul>")
    				open--
    			}
    			b.WriteString("</li><li>")
    		}
    		fmt.Fprintf(&b, `<a href="#%s">%s</a>`, h.id, h.text)
    	}
    	for ; open > 0; open-- {
    		b.WriteString("</li></ul>")
    	}
    	b.WriteString("</nav>")
    	fmt.Fprintf(&b, `<a class="goto-toc" href="#%s">Back to contents</a>`, id)
    	return b.String()
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Lists and tables}

#lists{
  The original barkdown's `#list{One, Two, Three}` separates items by commas, and that is kept. `#ul` and `#ol` separate
  items by lines, and a line may begin with a bullet such as `-`, `*` or `1.`, which is dropped, so a list can be
  pasted from Markdown. When the items need several paragraphs, explicit `#li{ ... }` bags can be used instead.

  The split happens on the tree, not on the rendered HTML as in the original barkdown, so a comma inside an inline code span
  or a link does not split an item. A line that holds nothing but a block bag, such as a nested `#ul`, is
  attached to the item above it, which is how lists nest.

  ```
  var bulletMark = regexp.MustCompile(`^\s*(?:[-*+•]|\d+[.)])\s+`)

  func (r *Renderer) list(b bt.Bag, el, sep string) string {
  	var items []string
  	if hasBag(b.Kids(), "li") {
  		for _, k := range b.Kids() {
  			if li, ok := k.(bt.Bag); ok && strings.TrimSpace(li.Tag()) == "li" {
  				items = append(items, r.compactFlow(li.Kids()))
  			}
  		}
  	} else {
  		for _, group := range splitNodes(b.Kids(), sep) {
  			group = trimNodes(group)
  			if len(group) == 0 {
  				continue
  			}
  			if sep == "\n" {
  				if first, ok := group[0].(bt.Blob); ok {
  					group[0] = bt.NewBlob(bulletMark.ReplaceAllString(first.Text(), ""))
  				}
  			}
  			if len(items) > 0 && isBlockBag(group) {
  				items[len(items)-1] += "\n" + r.inline(group)
  				continue
  			}
  			items = append(items, r.inline(group))
  		}
  	}
  	var out strings.Builder
  	out.WriteString("<" + el + ">\n")
  	for _, it := range items {
  		out.WriteString("<li>" + it + "</li>\n")
  	}
  	out.WriteString("</" + el + ">")
  	return out.String()
  }
  ```

  #node list helpers{
    `splitNodes` cuts a node list at every occurrence of a separator inside its blobs, leaving bags and code
    whole. `trimNodes` removes white space at the two ends of a piece. Neither modifies the tree: they build new
    blobs, because a node is shared by every copy of it.

    ```
    // splitNodes splits nodes at every sep inside a blob; other nodes stay whole.
    func splitNodes(nodes []bt.Node, sep string) [][]bt.Node {
    	groups := [][]bt.Node{nil}
    	for _, n := range nodes {
    		blob, ok := n.(bt.Blob)
    		if !ok {
    			groups[len(groups)-1] = append(groups[len(groups)-1], n)
    			continue
    		}
    		for i, part := range strings.Split(blob.Text(), sep) {
    			if i > 0 {
    				groups = append(groups, nil)
    			}
    			if part != "" {
    				groups[len(groups)-1] = append(groups[len(groups)-1], bt.NewBlob(part))
    			}
    		}
    	}
    	return groups
    }

    // trimNodes removes white space at both ends of a node list.
    func trimNodes(nodes []bt.Node) []bt.Node {
    	blank := func(n bt.Node) bool {
    		b, ok := n.(bt.Blob)
    		return ok && strings.TrimSpace(b.Text()) == ""
    	}
    	for len(nodes) > 0 && blank(nodes[0]) {
    		nodes = nodes[1:]
    	}
    	for len(nodes) > 0 && blank(nodes[len(nodes)-1]) {
    		nodes = nodes[:len(nodes)-1]
    	}
    	if len(nodes) == 0 {
    		return nil
    	}
    	out := append([]bt.Node(nil), nodes...)
    	if b, ok := out[0].(bt.Blob); ok {
    		out[0] = bt.NewBlob(strings.TrimLeftFunc(b.Text(), unicode.IsSpace))
    	}
    	if b, ok := out[len(out)-1].(bt.Blob); ok {
    		out[len(out)-1] = bt.NewBlob(strings.TrimRightFunc(b.Text(), unicode.IsSpace))
    	}
    	return out
    }

    func hasBag(nodes []bt.Node, tag string) bool {
    	for _, n := range nodes {
    		if b, ok := n.(bt.Bag); ok && strings.TrimSpace(b.Tag()) == tag {
    			return true
    		}
    	}
    	return false
    }

    // isBlockBag reports whether nodes is a single bag that renders as a block.
    func isBlockBag(nodes []bt.Node) bool {
    	if len(nodes) != 1 {
    		return false
    	}
    	b, ok := nodes[0].(bt.Bag)
    	return ok && handlers[strings.TrimSpace(b.Tag())].block
    }

    func words(nodes []bt.Node) string {
    	return strings.Join(strings.Fields(textOfNodes(nodes)), " ")
    }
    ```
  }
}

#tables{
  A table is written one row per line with cells separated by `|`, as in Markdown. A row of dashes after the
  first row makes that row the header; leading and trailing pipes are optional. A `#caption{}` anywhere in the
  table becomes its caption, and for full control `#tr`, `#th` and `#td` bags may be used instead of lines. The
  table is wrapped in a `div` so that the stylesheet can let wide tables scroll sideways on a phone.

  ```
  var separatorRow = regexp.MustCompile(`^\|?\s*:?-{3,}:?\s*(\|\s*:?-{3,}:?\s*)*\|?$`)

  func (r *Renderer) table(b bt.Bag) string {
  	var caption string
  	var kids []bt.Node
  	for _, k := range b.Kids() {
  		if c, ok := k.(bt.Bag); ok && strings.TrimSpace(c.Tag()) == "caption" {
  			caption = r.inline(c.Kids())
  			continue
  		}
  		kids = append(kids, k)
  	}
  	var out strings.Builder
  	out.WriteString(`<div class="table"><table>`)
  	if caption != "" {
  		out.WriteString("<caption>" + caption + "</caption>")
  	}
  	if hasBag(kids, "tr") {
  		out.WriteString(r.inline(kids))
  	} else {
  		rows, header := r.tableRows(kids)
  		if header {
  			out.WriteString("<thead>" + tableRow(rows[0], "th") + "</thead>")
  			rows = rows[1:]
  		}
  		out.WriteString("<tbody>")
  		for _, row := range rows {
  			out.WriteString(tableRow(row, "td"))
  		}
  		out.WriteString("</tbody>")
  	}
  	out.WriteString("</table></div>")
  	return out.String()
  }
  ```

  #table rows{
    ```
    func (r *Renderer) tableRows(nodes []bt.Node) (rows [][]string, header bool) {
    	for _, line := range splitNodes(nodes, "\n") {
    		line = trimNodes(line)
    		if len(line) == 0 {
    			continue
    		}
    		plain := textOfNodes(line)
    		if separatorRow.MatchString(plain) {
    			if len(rows) == 1 {
    				header = true
    			}
    			continue
    		}
    		cells := splitNodes(line, "|")
    		if strings.HasPrefix(plain, "|") && len(cells) > 1 {
    			cells = cells[1:]
    		}
    		if strings.HasSuffix(plain, "|") && len(cells) > 1 {
    			cells = cells[:len(cells)-1]
    		}
    		var row []string
    		for _, c := range cells {
    			row = append(row, r.inline(trimNodes(c)))
    		}
    		rows = append(rows, row)
    	}
    	return rows, header
    }

    func tableRow(cells []string, el string) string {
    	var b strings.Builder
    	b.WriteString("<tr>")
    	for _, c := range cells {
    		b.WriteString("<" + el + ">" + c + "</" + el + ">")
    	}
    	b.WriteString("</tr>\n")
    	return b.String()
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Links, images and media}

#register links and media{
  ```
  handlers["link"] = handler{false, (*Renderer).link}
  handlers["a"] = handler{false, (*Renderer).link}
  handlers["ref"] = handler{false, (*Renderer).ref}
  handlers["img"] = handler{false, (*Renderer).image}
  handlers["image"] = handler{false, (*Renderer).image}
  handlers["figure"] = handler{true, (*Renderer).figure}
  handlers["video"] = handler{true, media("video")}
  handlers["audio"] = handler{true, media("audio")}
  handlers["youtube"] = handler{true, (*Renderer).youtube}
  handlers["abbr"] = handler{false, (*Renderer).abbr}
  ```
}

#first word{
  Links, images and abbreviations share one convention: the first word of the bag is the target and the rest is
  the text, as in `#link{https://go.dev the Go website}`. A URL with a fragment contains a `#`, which brashtag
  reserves, so the first word may also be given between backticks.

  ```
  // firstWord splits off the first word of a bag's content.
  func firstWord(nodes []bt.Node) (string, []bt.Node) {
  	for i, n := range nodes {
  		switch x := n.(type) {
  		case bt.Blob:
  			t := strings.TrimLeftFunc(x.Text(), unicode.IsSpace)
  			if t == "" {
  				continue
  			}
  			j := strings.IndexFunc(t, unicode.IsSpace)
  			if j < 0 {
  				return t, nodes[i+1:]
  			}
  			return t[:j], append([]bt.Node{bt.NewBlob(t[j:])}, nodes[i+1:]...)
  		case bt.Code:
  			return x.Text(), nodes[i+1:]
  		default:
  			return "", nodes[i:]
  		}
  	}
  	return "", nil
  }

  func isExternal(u string) bool {
  	return strings.Contains(u, "://") || strings.HasPrefix(u, "//") ||
  		strings.HasPrefix(u, "mailto:") || strings.HasPrefix(u, "tel:")
  }

  func (r *Renderer) url(p string) string {
  	if r.site != nil {
  		return r.site.URL(p)
  	}
  	return p
  }
  ```
}

#links{
  A link that starts with `/` is relative to the site, and the site adds its base path, so the same post works
  whether the site lives at the root of a domain or under `/blog/`. External links are marked with a class and
  `rel="noopener"`.

  `#ref{slug}` links to another post or page by its slug and borrows its title as the link text. A typo there is
  caught at build time with a warning, instead of becoming a broken link that someone finds months later. A
  fragment may follow the slug: ``#ref{`hello#tables`}``.

  ```
  func (r *Renderer) link(b bt.Bag) string {
  	href, rest := firstWord(b.Kids())
  	label := r.inline(rest)
  	if href == "" {
  		r.warnf("#link{} without a URL")
  		return label
  	}
  	if label == "" {
  		label = html.EscapeString(href)
  	}
  	attrs := ""
  	if isExternal(href) {
  		attrs = ` class="external" rel="noopener"`
  	}
  	return fmt.Sprintf(`<a href="%s"%s>%s</a>`, html.EscapeString(r.url(href)), attrs, label)
  }

  func (r *Renderer) ref(b bt.Bag) string {
  	slug, rest := firstWord(b.Kids())
  	label := r.inline(rest)
  	frag := ""
  	if i := strings.IndexByte(slug, '#'); i >= 0 {
  		slug, frag = slug[:i], slug[i:]
  	}
  	var target *Doc
  	if r.site != nil {
  		target = r.site.bySlug[slug]
  	}
  	if target == nil {
  		r.warnf("#ref{%s}: no post or page has this slug", slug)
  		if label == "" {
  			label = html.EscapeString(slug)
  		}
  		return `<a class="broken">` + label + `</a>`
  	}
  	if label == "" {
  		label = html.EscapeString(target.Title)
  	}
  	return fmt.Sprintf(`<a href="%s">%s</a>`, html.EscapeString(r.url(target.URL)+frag), label)
  }
  ```
}

#images and media{
  Images are lazy-loaded. `#img` is inline and its text is the `alt` text; `#figure` is a block whose text is a
  caption, which also serves as the `alt` text in plain form. Video and audio use the browser's own controls.
  YouTube videos are embedded from the privacy-enhanced `youtube-nocookie.com` domain, which does not set
  cookies until the reader plays the video.

  ```
  func (r *Renderer) image(b bt.Bag) string {
  	src, rest := firstWord(b.Kids())
  	return fmt.Sprintf(`<img src="%s" alt="%s" loading="lazy">`,
  		html.EscapeString(r.url(src)), html.EscapeString(words(rest)))
  }

  func (r *Renderer) figure(b bt.Bag) string {
  	src, rest := firstWord(b.Kids())
  	caption := r.inline(rest)
  	if caption != "" {
  		caption = "<figcaption>" + caption + "</figcaption>"
  	}
  	return fmt.Sprintf(`<figure><img src="%s" alt="%s" loading="lazy">%s</figure>`,
  		html.EscapeString(r.url(src)), html.EscapeString(words(rest)), caption)
  }

  func media(el string) func(*Renderer, bt.Bag) string {
  	return func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  		src, rest := firstWord(b.Kids())
  		out := fmt.Sprintf(`<figure class="media"><%s controls preload="metadata" src="%s"></%s>`,
  			el, html.EscapeString(r.url(src)), el)
  		if caption := r.inline(rest); caption != "" {
  			out += "<figcaption>" + caption + "</figcaption>"
  		}
  		return out + "</figure>"
  	}
  }

  var youtubeID = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{6,}$`)

  func (r *Renderer) youtube(b bt.Bag) string {
  	id, rest := firstWord(b.Kids())
  	if !youtubeID.MatchString(id) {
  		r.warnf("#youtube{%s}: not a video id", id)
  		return ""
  	}
  	title := words(rest)
  	if title == "" {
  		title = "YouTube video"
  	}
  	return fmt.Sprintf(`<div class="embed"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/%s" title="%s" `+
  		`loading="lazy" allow="encrypted-media; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>`,
  		id, html.EscapeString(title))
  }

  func (r *Renderer) abbr(b bt.Bag) string {
  	word, rest := firstWord(b.Kids())
  	return fmt.Sprintf(`<abbr title="%s">%s</abbr>`, html.EscapeString(words(rest)), html.EscapeString(word))
  }
  ```
}

#h2{Sidenotes, call-outs and details}

#register notes and asides{
  ```
  handlers["fn"] = handler{false, (*Renderer).footnote}
  handlers["footnote"] = handler{false, (*Renderer).footnote}
  handlers["sidenote"] = handler{false, (*Renderer).footnote}
  handlers["marginnote"] = handler{false, (*Renderer).marginnote}
  handlers["newthought"] = handler{false, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  	return `<span class="newthought">` + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</span>"
  }}
  handlers["epigraph"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  	return "<div class=\"epigraph\"><blockquote>\n" + r.flow(b.Kids()) + "</blockquote></div>"
  }}
  handlers["details"] = handler{true, (*Renderer).details}
  handlers["thought"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return r.timeline([]bt.Node{b}) }}
  handlers["tweet"] = handlers["thought"]
  handlers["summary"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  	return "<summary>" + r.inline(b.Kids()) + "</summary>"
  }}
  for _, c := range [][2]string{{"note", "Note"}, {"tip", "Tip"}, {"info", "Info"},
  	{"warning", "Warning"}, {"danger", "Danger"}, {"important", "Important"}} {
  	handlers[c[0]] = callout(c[0], c[1])
  }
  ```
}

#footnotes{
  Notes live in the margin, beside the line that mentions them, the way Edward Tufte sets his books
  #cite{tufte2006beautiful} and the way Tufte CSS brings that layout to the web #cite{tuftecss}. A reader's eye
  moves a few centimetres instead of jumping to the bottom of the page and back. `#fn{like this}` (also
  `#sidenote`) makes a numbered sidenote; `#marginnote{...}` makes one without a number, for remarks and
  captions.

  The markup is the one Tufte CSS uses. The note itself is a `span`, because it must sit inside the paragraph
  to float beside it. On a narrow screen there is no margin, so the notes are hidden and the number becomes a
  `label` for a hidden checkbox: tapping it checks the box and the stylesheet shows the note under the line,
  with no script. Unlike Tufte CSS, the numbers are written into the HTML rather than produced by CSS counters,
  so they survive in feed readers, which ignore the stylesheet. A note's number is taken before its content is
  rendered, so a note inside a note gets the next number. An excerpt on the home page has no margin to spare,
  so there notes disappear.

  ```
  func (r *Renderer) footnote(b bt.Bag) string {
  	if r.excerpt {
  		return ""
  	}
  	r.notes++
  	n := r.notes
  	return fmt.Sprintf(`<label for="sn-%d" class="margin-toggle sidenote-number">%d</label>`+
  		`<input type="checkbox" id="sn-%d" class="margin-toggle">`+
  		`<span class="sidenote"><span class="sidenote-mark">%d</span> %s</span>`, n, n, n, n, r.inline(b.Kids()))
  }

  func (r *Renderer) marginnote(b bt.Bag) string {
  	if r.excerpt {
  		return ""
  	}
  	r.margins++
  	n := r.margins
  	return fmt.Sprintf(`<label for="mn-%d" class="margin-toggle" aria-label="Show note">&#8853;</label>`+
  		`<input type="checkbox" id="mn-%d" class="margin-toggle">`+
  		`<span class="marginnote">%s</span>`, n, n, r.inline(b.Kids()))
  }
  ```

  `#newthought{...}` opens a new section with its first words in small capitals, Tufte's alternative to a
  heading for a change of subject that does not need one, and `#epigraph{...}` sets a quotation at the head of a
  piece.
}

#callouts and details{
  Call-outs (`#note`, `#tip`, `#warning` and friends) are boxes with a label; a `#title{}` inside replaces the
  label. `#details` makes a collapsible section whose `#summary{}` is the line that stays visible.

  ```
  func callout(kind, label string) handler {
  	return handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  		title := label
  		var kids []bt.Node
  		for _, k := range b.Kids() {
  			if t, ok := k.(bt.Bag); ok && strings.TrimSpace(t.Tag()) == "title" {
  				title = r.inline(t.Kids())
  				continue
  			}
  			kids = append(kids, k)
  		}
  		return fmt.Sprintf(`<aside class="callout callout-%s"><p class="callout-title">%s</p>%s</aside>`,
  			kind, title, r.flow(kids))
  	}}
  }

  func (r *Renderer) details(b bt.Bag) string {
  	summary := "Details"
  	var kids []bt.Node
  	for _, k := range b.Kids() {
  		if s, ok := k.(bt.Bag); ok && strings.TrimSpace(s.Tag()) == "summary" {
  			summary = r.inline(s.Kids())
  			continue
  		}
  		kids = append(kids, k)
  	}
  	return "<details><summary>" + summary + "</summary>\n" + r.flow(kids) + "</details>"
  }
  ```
}

#h2{Code, math and diagrams}

#register code, math and diagrams{
  ```
  handlers["code"] = handler{false, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  	return "<code>" + html.EscapeString(textOf(b)) + "</code>"
  }}
  handlers["pre"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  	return "<pre>" + html.EscapeString(dedent(textOf(b))) + "</pre>"
  }}
  handlers["html"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string { return textOf(b) }}
  handlers["math"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  	r.math = true
  	return `<div class="math">\[` + html.EscapeString(strings.TrimSpace(textOf(b))) + `\]</div>`
  }}
  handlers["mermaid"] = handler{true, func(r *Renderer, b bt.Bag) string {
  	r.mermaid = true
  	return `<pre class="mermaid">` + html.EscapeString(dedent(textOf(b))) + "</pre>"
  }}
  ```

  `#html` passes its content through unescaped, for the rare embed that barkdown has no tag for; put the HTML
  between backticks. `#math` and `#mermaid` mark the page so that the template loads MathJax or Mermaid, and
  only on that page. Escaping their content is safe because both libraries read the decoded text of the
  element.
}

#code blocks{
  A code block may name its language on the line of its opening backticks, as in Markdown. The language line
  must be a single word and must be followed by more lines, so a one-line block that happens to be one word
  is not mistaken for a language. The code is then dedented: the indentation all its lines share is removed,
  so a block can be indented together with the bag it sits in, as in the original barkdown's demo, without the indentation
  showing up in the page.

  Blocks in the languages `math` and `mermaid` are the same as the bags of those names. Others are
  highlighted. Inside a literate chunk the block is labelled with the chunk's name, written just above the
  code the way the original barkdown labels it with the enclosing tag. The language goes in a separate
  `code-head`, which the theme sets in the margin.

  ```
  func isFence(c bt.Code) bool { return len(c.Tag()) >= 3 }

  var langName = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_+#.-]*$`)

  func splitLang(text string) (lang, code string) {
  	if nl := strings.IndexByte(text, '\n'); nl > 0 && langName.MatchString(text[:nl]) {
  		return strings.ToLower(text[:nl]), text[nl+1:]
  	}
  	return "", strings.TrimPrefix(text, "\n")
  }

  func (r *Renderer) fence(c bt.Code) string {
  	lang, code := splitLang(c.Text())
  	code = dedent(code)
  	switch lang {
  	case "math", "latex", "tex":
  		r.math = true
  		return `<div class="math">\[` + html.EscapeString(code) + `\]</div>`
  	case "mermaid":
  		r.mermaid = true
  		return `<pre class="mermaid">` + html.EscapeString(code) + "</pre>"
  	}
  	label, head, class := "", "", ""
  	if r.chunk != "" {
  		label = fmt.Sprintf(`<div class="chunk-label"><a class="chunk-name" href="#%s">⟨%s⟩</a></div>`,
  			chunkID(r.chunk), html.EscapeString(r.chunk))
  	}
  	if lang != "" {
  		head = fmt.Sprintf(`<div class="code-head"><span class="lang">%s</span></div>`, html.EscapeString(lang))
  		class = fmt.Sprintf(` class="language-%s"`, html.EscapeString(lang))
  	}
  	return fmt.Sprintf(`<div class="codeblock">%s%s<pre><code%s>%s</code></pre></div>`,
  		label, head, class, r.codeHTML(code, lang))
  }
  ```

  #dedent{
    Blank lines at either end are dropped. The common indentation is the longest prefix of white space shared
    by all non-blank lines; a mixture of tabs and spaces simply shortens it.

    ```
    // dedent removes the indentation common to all non-blank lines and the
    // blank lines around the code.
    func dedent(s string) string {
    	lines := strings.Split(s, "\n")
    	for len(lines) > 0 && strings.TrimSpace(lines[0]) == "" {
    		lines = lines[1:]
    	}
    	for len(lines) > 0 && strings.TrimSpace(lines[len(lines)-1]) == "" {
    		lines = lines[:len(lines)-1]
    	}
    	prefix, first := "", true
    	for _, l := range lines {
    		if strings.TrimSpace(l) == "" {
    			continue
    		}
    		ws := l[:len(l)-len(strings.TrimLeft(l, " \t"))]
    		if first {
    			prefix, first = ws, false
    			continue
    		}
    		for !strings.HasPrefix(ws, prefix) {
    			prefix = prefix[:len(prefix)-1]
    		}
    	}
    	for i, l := range lines {
    		if strings.TrimSpace(l) == "" {
    			lines[i] = ""
    		} else {
    			lines[i] = l[len(prefix):]
    		}
    	}
    	return strings.Join(lines, "\n")
    }
    ```
  }

  #code with chunk links{
    A line consisting of `<<<name>>>` that names a chunk of this document becomes a link to it. The triple
    angle brackets are only how the writer marks the reference; on the page it is typeset the way Knuth's WEB
    typesets one #cite{knuth1984lp}, the chunk's name in angle brackets, ⟨like this⟩, matching the label above
    the chunk itself, so that a reader sees a phrase standing in for code rather than syntax. The highlighter
    must not see the reference, or it would colour the name's words as identifiers and escape the angle
    brackets in the middle of a link. So each reference line is replaced by one character from a private-use
    plane of Unicode, which the highlighter passes through untouched as an unknown symbol, and the link is put in
    its place afterwards. Plane 15 offers 65,534 such characters, more references than any block will hold.

    ```
    var refLine = regexp.MustCompile(`^(\s*)<<<(.+?)>>>\s*$`)

    func (r *Renderer) codeHTML(code, lang string) string {
    	var refs []string
    	lines := strings.Split(code, "\n")
    	for i, l := range lines {
    		if m := refLine.FindStringSubmatch(l); m != nil && r.chunks[m[2]] {
    			lines[i] = m[1] + string(rune(0xF0000+len(refs)))
    			refs = append(refs, m[2])
    		}
    	}
    	out := Highlight(strings.Join(lines, "\n"), lang)
    	for i, name := range refs {
    		link := fmt.Sprintf(`<a class="chunkref" href="#%s">⟨%s⟩</a>`,
    			chunkID(name), html.EscapeString(name))
    		out = strings.Replace(out, string(rune(0xF0000+i)), link, 1)
    	}
    	return out
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Thoughts and timelines}

Some posts are never finished. A writer keeps a topic open and adds a thought whenever one occurs, the way
people post short notes to a social network, but in their own post and their own place. Each thought is a
bag that starts with its timestamp:

#sample thought{
  ````
  #thought{2026-11-11 09:30
    The margin is the best part of Tufte's layout.
  }
  ````
}

`#tweet` is accepted as another name for the same thing. The time of day may be left out. Everything after the
timestamp is an ordinary flow, so a thought may hold paragraphs, code, links and notes.

#thought type{
  A `Thought` is what the rest of the program needs to know about one: when it was written, whether the time of
  day was given, its anchor, and its body as HTML and as text, for feeds and search.

  ```
  // Thought is one timestamped entry of a post that grows over time.
  type Thought struct {
  	Time  time.Time
  	Clock bool // the timestamp includes a time of day
  	ID    string
  	HTML  template.HTML
  	Text  string
  }

  func isThought(n bt.Node) bool {
  	b, ok := n.(bt.Bag)
  	if !ok {
  		return false
  	}
  	tag := strings.TrimSpace(b.Tag())
  	return tag == "thought" || tag == "tweet"
  }

  // thoughtRun returns the end of the run of thoughts that starts at i. White
  // space between two thoughts belongs to the run; anything else ends it.
  func thoughtRun(nodes []bt.Node, i int) int {
  	end := i
  	for j := i; j < len(nodes); j++ {
  		if isThought(nodes[j]) {
  			end = j + 1
  			continue
  		}
  		if b, ok := nodes[j].(bt.Blob); ok && strings.TrimSpace(b.Text()) == "" {
  			continue
  		}
  		break
  	}
  	return end
  }
  ```
}

#parse a timestamp{
  The timestamp is the first word of the bag, a date, and optionally a second word that looks like a clock.
  The two are read by the same `parseDate` as `#date`, in the writer's time zone. A thought whose timestamp
  cannot be read is still shown, with a warning, so that a typo never loses words.

  ```
  var clockWord = regexp.MustCompile(`^\d{1,2}:\d{2}$`)

  func (r *Renderer) parseThought(b bt.Bag) (*Thought, []bt.Node) {
  	t := &Thought{}
  	day, rest := firstWord(b.Kids())
  	stamp := day
  	if clock, after := firstWord(rest); clockWord.MatchString(clock) {
  		stamp, rest, t.Clock = day+" "+clock, after, true
  	}
  	when, err := parseDate(stamp)
  	if err != nil {
  		r.warnf("#thought{%s ...}: a thought starts with its date, such as 2026-11-11 or 2026-11-11 09:30", stamp)
  		return t, b.Kids()
  	}
  	t.Time = when
  	return t, rest
  }

  func (r *Renderer) stamp(t *Thought) string {
  	layout := "2 January 2006"
  	if r.site != nil {
  		layout = r.site.DateFormat
  	}
  	return formatStamp(t, layout)
  }

  func formatStamp(t *Thought, layout string) string {
  	if t.Time.IsZero() {
  		return "undated"
  	}
  	if t.Clock {
  		return t.Time.Format(layout + ", 15:04")
  	}
  	return t.Time.Format(layout)
  }
  ```
}

#timelines{
  A run of thoughts is shown as one timeline. They are sorted by time, whatever order they were typed in, so a
  thought remembered late can be added where it is convenient. The order is oldest first, the order of a diary,
  unless the post says `#order{newest}`; either way the reader can flip it, which the theme's script does by
  reversing the elements. The sort is stable, so thoughts with the same timestamp keep the order they were
  written in.

  Each thought gets an anchor made from its timestamp, `t-2026-11-11-0930`, which stays the same when other
  thoughts are added, so a link to a thought keeps working. Its timestamp is itself a link to that anchor. The
  thoughts are collected for the site, which uses them for the post's date of last change, the timeline page and
  a feed of thoughts.

  ```
  func (r *Renderer) timeline(nodes []bt.Node) string {
  	type item struct {
  		t    *Thought
  		body []bt.Node
  	}
  	var items []item
  	for _, n := range nodes {
  		if isThought(n) {
  			t, body := r.parseThought(n.(bt.Bag))
  			items = append(items, item{t, body})
  		}
  	}
  	sort.SliceStable(items, func(i, j int) bool { return items[i].t.Time.Before(items[j].t.Time) })
  	order := "oldest"
  	if r.newest {
  		order = "newest"
  		for i, j := 0, len(items)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
  			items[i], items[j] = items[j], items[i]
  		}
  	}
  	var b strings.Builder
  	fmt.Fprintf(&b, "<section class=\"timeline\" data-order=\"%s\">\n", order)
  	count := "1 thought"
  	if len(items) != 1 {
  		count = fmt.Sprintf("%d thoughts", len(items))
  	}
  	fmt.Fprintf(&b, "<p class=\"timeline-head\"><span>%s</span></p>\n", count)
  	for _, it := range items {
  		t := it.t
  		id := "t"
  		if !t.Time.IsZero() {
  			id = "t-" + t.Time.Format("2006-01-02")
  			if t.Clock {
  				id += t.Time.Format("-1504")
  			}
  		}
  		t.ID = r.uniqueID(id)
  		body := r.flow(it.body)
  		t.HTML, t.Text = template.HTML(body), plainText(it.body)
  		if !r.excerpt {
  			r.thoughts = append(r.thoughts, t)
  		}
  		fmt.Fprintf(&b, "<article class=\"thought\" id=\"%s\">\n<p class=\"thought-stamp\"><a href=\"#%s\"><time datetime=\"%s\">%s</time></a></p>\n%s</article>\n",
  			t.ID, t.ID, t.Time.Format(time.RFC3339), html.EscapeString(r.stamp(t)), body)
  	}
  	b.WriteString("</section>")
  	return b.String()
  }
  ```
}

#h2{Unknown tags and literate posts}

#unknown tags{
  A tag that is not in the table is either a mistake or a name. Brashtag invites writers to invent tags, and
  the original barkdown treats any bag that holds a code block as a named piece of code: the block gets the
  bag's name as its label and `id`, and `<<<name>>>` lines elsewhere link to it. That makes a post a small
  literate program, and this barkdown does the same. `findChunks` first collects every unknown tag that directly holds a code block;
  such a bag is rendered as a `div` with the chunk's anchor, its blocks labelled.

  Any other unknown tag is rendered as an element with the class `bt-` plus its name, so a stylesheet can give
  it meaning, and the build warns about it in case it was a typo. Whether it becomes a block `div` or an inline
  `span` depends on what it contains. An empty tag, the `#{ ... }` group, is simply a flow.

  ```
  func chunkID(name string) string { return "chunk-" + slugify(name) }

  func (r *Renderer) findChunks(nodes []bt.Node) {
  	for _, n := range nodes {
  		b, ok := n.(bt.Bag)
  		if !ok {
  			continue
  		}
  		tag := strings.TrimSpace(b.Tag())
  		if _, known := handlers[tag]; !known && tag != "" && hasFence(b.Kids()) {
  			r.chunks[tag] = true
  		}
  		r.findChunks(b.Kids())
  	}
  }

  func (r *Renderer) unknown(b bt.Bag) (string, bool) {
  	tag := strings.TrimSpace(b.Tag())
  	switch {
  	case tag == "":
  		return r.flow(b.Kids()), true
  	case r.chunks[tag]:
  		outer := r.chunk
  		r.chunk = tag
  		inner := r.flow(b.Kids())
  		r.chunk = outer
  		return fmt.Sprintf(`<div class="chunk" id="%s">%s</div>`, r.uniqueID(chunkID(tag)), inner), true
  	}
  	r.warnf("unknown tag #%s{}, rendered with class bt-%s", tag, slugify(tag))
  	class := "bt-" + slugify(tag)
  	if hasBlock(b.Kids()) {
  		return fmt.Sprintf(`<div class="%s">%s</div>`, class, r.flow(b.Kids())), true
  	}
  	return fmt.Sprintf(`<span class="%s">%s</span>`, class, r.inline(b.Kids())), false
  }
  ```

  #block content tests{
    ```
    func hasFence(nodes []bt.Node) bool {
    	for _, n := range nodes {
    		if c, ok := n.(bt.Code); ok && isFence(c) {
    			return true
    		}
    	}
    	return false
    }

    // hasBlock reports whether nodes contain a paragraph break or a block.
    func hasBlock(nodes []bt.Node) bool {
    	for _, n := range nodes {
    		switch x := n.(type) {
    		case bt.Blob:
    			if blankLine.MatchString(x.Text()) {
    				return true
    			}
    		case bt.Code:
    			if isFence(x) {
    				return true
    			}
    		case bt.Bag:
    			if h, ok := handlers[strings.TrimSpace(x.Tag())]; !ok || h.block {
    				return true
    			}
    		}
    	}
    	return false
    }
    ```
  }
}
#h1{Syntax highlighting}

A highlighter does not need a parser. Readers want comments, strings, keywords and numbers to stand out, and
those are all recognisable from a few characters of context. So each language is described by a small table:
its keywords, some well-known built-in names, how comments and strings start, and a few quirks. One scanner
serves every table. It is greedy and linear, one pass over the code, and it never fails: anything it does not
recognise is copied through, escaped.

#h2{Describing a language}

#language type{
  `rawQuotes` lists the quote characters whose strings have no escapes, such as Go's backquote and the shell's
  single quote. `triple` enables Python's triple-quoted strings. `variables` colours the shell's `$name` and
  `${name}`. `foldCase` makes keyword matching case-insensitive, for SQL.

  ```
  // language describes enough of a language's lexical structure to colour it.
  type language struct {
  	keywords     map[string]bool
  	builtins     map[string]bool
  	lineComment  []string
  	blockComment [][2]string
  	quotes       string // characters that open a string
  	rawQuotes    string // of those, the ones whose strings have no escapes
  	triple       bool   // """ and ''' strings
  	variables    bool   // $name and ${name}
  	foldCase     bool   // keywords are case-insensitive
  }

  func wordSet(words string) map[string]bool {
  	m := map[string]bool{}
  	for _, w := range strings.Fields(words) {
  		m[w] = true
  	}
  	return m
  }

  var languages = map[string]*language{}
  ```
}

#language tables{
  The tables follow. Each language is registered under the names people write after the opening backticks.
  The C family shares one table, which is a union of C, C++, Java and C-sharp keywords; a word that is a keyword
  in one of them and an identifier in another is rare enough not to matter. Rust gets a copy without the single
  quote, which starts lifetimes as often as character literals.

  ```
  func init() {
  	register := func(l *language, names ...string) {
  		for _, n := range names {
  			languages[n] = l
  		}
  	}
  	slashes := []string{"//"}
  	cblock := [][2]string{{"/*", "*/"}}
  	hash := []string{"#"}

  	register(&language{
  		keywords: wordSet(`break case chan const continue default defer else fallthrough for func go goto
  			if import interface map package range return select struct switch type var`),
  		builtins: wordSet(`append cap clear close complex copy delete imag len make max min new panic print
  			println real recover any bool byte comparable complex64 complex128 error float32 float64 int
  			int8 int16 int32 int64 rune string uint uint8 uint16 uint32 uint64 uintptr true false nil iota`),
  		lineComment: slashes, blockComment: cblock, quotes: "\"'`", rawQuotes: "`",
  	}, "go", "golang")

  	register(&language{
  		keywords: wordSet(`and as assert async await break class continue def del elif else except finally
  			for from global if import in is lambda nonlocal not or pass raise return try while with yield
  			match case`),
  		builtins: wordSet(`True False None self print len range int str float list dict set tuple bool
  			open super isinstance enumerate zip map filter sorted sum min max abs any all type object
  			Exception ValueError KeyError`),
  		lineComment: hash, quotes: `"'`, triple: true,
  	}, "python", "py", "python3")

  	register(&language{
  		keywords: wordSet(`break case catch class const continue debugger default delete do else export
  			extends finally for function if import in instanceof let new of return static super switch this
  			throw try typeof var void while with yield async await from as interface type enum implements`),
  		builtins: wordSet(`true false null undefined NaN Infinity console document window Math JSON Object
  			Array String Number Boolean Promise Map Set Error fetch`),
  		lineComment: slashes, blockComment: cblock, quotes: "\"'`",
  	}, "javascript", "js", "typescript", "ts", "jsx", "tsx", "mjs")

  	cfamily := &language{
  		keywords: wordSet(`auto break case char const continue default do double else enum extern float for
  			goto if inline int long register return short signed sizeof static struct switch typedef union
  			unsigned void volatile while class public private protected new delete this namespace using
  			template typename virtual override try catch throw bool true false nullptr null import package
  			extends implements interface final abstract boolean fn let mut impl trait pub use mod match loop
  			where self Self crate move ref as dyn unsafe async await`),
  		builtins: wordSet(`printf malloc free size_t uint8_t uint32_t int64_t std string vector cout endl
  			String System Vec Option Some None Ok Err Box println`),
  		lineComment: slashes, blockComment: cblock, quotes: `"'`,
  	}
  	register(cfamily, "c", "h", "cpp", "c++", "cc", "hpp", "java", "cs", "csharp", "kotlin", "swift", "zig")
  	rust := *cfamily
  	rust.quotes = `"`
  	register(&rust, "rust", "rs")

  	register(&language{
  		keywords: wordSet(`if then else elif fi for while until do done case esac in function return exit
  			export local readonly set unset shift break continue`),
  		builtins: wordSet(`echo cd ls cat grep sed awk printf read test source alias go git make curl sudo
  			mkdir rm cp mv chmod`),
  		lineComment: hash, quotes: `"'`, rawQuotes: "'", variables: true,
  	}, "sh", "bash", "zsh", "shell", "console")

  	register(&language{
  		keywords: wordSet(`select from where insert into values update set delete create table drop alter
  			index join left right inner outer on group by order having limit offset as and or not null is in
  			like between distinct union all primary key foreign references default case when then else end
  			with returning`),
  		builtins:    wordSet(`count sum avg min max coalesce integer text varchar boolean timestamp`),
  		lineComment: []string{"--"}, blockComment: cblock, quotes: `'"`, foldCase: true,
  	}, "sql")

  	register(&language{keywords: wordSet(`true false null`), quotes: `"`}, "json")
  	register(&language{
  		keywords:    wordSet(`true false null yes no on off`),
  		lineComment: hash, quotes: `"'`,
  	}, "yaml", "yml", "toml", "ini", "conf")
  	register(&language{blockComment: cblock, quotes: `"'`}, "css", "scss")
  }
  ```
}

#h2{The scanner}

#highlight{
  `Highlight` repeatedly asks the language for the next token's length and class, and wraps classified
  tokens in a `span` whose class the stylesheet colours: `tok-k` keywords, `tok-b` built-ins, `tok-s` strings,
  `tok-c` comments, `tok-n` numbers, `tok-f` function calls and `tok-v` variables. An unknown language is just
  escaped. Brashtag has its own small scanner below.

  ```
  // Highlight returns code as HTML, with its tokens in <span class="tok-X">.
  func Highlight(code, lang string) string {
  	if lang == "bt" || lang == "brashtag" {
  		return highlightBrashtag(code)
  	}
  	l := languages[lang]
  	if l == nil {
  		return html.EscapeString(code)
  	}
  	var b strings.Builder
  	for i := 0; i < len(code); {
  		n, class := l.token(code[i:])
  		if class == "" {
  			b.WriteString(html.EscapeString(code[i : i+n]))
  		} else {
  			span(&b, class, code[i:i+n])
  		}
  		i += n
  	}
  	return b.String()
  }

  func span(b *strings.Builder, class, text string) {
  	b.WriteString(`<span class="tok-` + class + `">` + html.EscapeString(text) + "</span>")
  }
  ```
}

#next token{
  The order of the tests matters. Comments are tried first, so that a quote inside a comment does not start a
  string, and triple quotes before single ones. A number is any run of word characters and dots starting with a
  digit, which covers `0x1F`, `1e9`, `1_000` and `3.14` without knowing their exact grammars. An identifier
  followed by an opening parenthesis is coloured as a function call. White space is consumed in runs, and any
  other character is returned on its own, whole, even when it takes several bytes in UTF-8.

  ```
  func (l *language) token(s string) (int, string) {
  	for _, p := range l.lineComment {
  		if strings.HasPrefix(s, p) {
  			if end := strings.IndexByte(s, '\n'); end >= 0 {
  				return end, "c"
  			}
  			return len(s), "c"
  		}
  	}
  	for _, p := range l.blockComment {
  		if strings.HasPrefix(s, p[0]) {
  			if end := strings.Index(s[len(p[0]):], p[1]); end >= 0 {
  				return len(p[0]) + end + len(p[1]), "c"
  			}
  			return len(s), "c"
  		}
  	}
  	if l.triple && (strings.HasPrefix(s, `"""`) || strings.HasPrefix(s, "'''")) {
  		if end := strings.Index(s[3:], s[:3]); end >= 0 {
  			return end + 6, "s"
  		}
  		return len(s), "s"
  	}
  	c := s[0]
  	if strings.IndexByte(l.quotes, c) >= 0 {
  		return scanString(s, strings.IndexByte(l.rawQuotes, c) >= 0), "s"
  	}
  	if l.variables && c == '$' && len(s) > 1 {
  		if s[1] == '{' {
  			if end := strings.IndexByte(s, '}'); end > 0 {
  				return end + 1, "v"
  			}
  		}
  		if n := identLen(s[1:]); n > 0 {
  			return n + 1, "v"
  		}
  	}
  	if c >= '0' && c <= '9' {
  		n := 1
  		for n < len(s) && (s[n] == '_' || s[n] == '.' || unicode.IsLetter(rune(s[n])) || unicode.IsDigit(rune(s[n]))) {
  			n++
  		}
  		return n, "n"
  	}
  	if n := identLen(s); n > 0 {
  		w := s[:n]
  		if l.foldCase {
  			w = strings.ToLower(w)
  		}
  		switch {
  		case l.keywords[w]:
  			return n, "k"
  		case l.builtins[w]:
  			return n, "b"
  		case strings.HasPrefix(strings.TrimLeft(s[n:], " "), "("):
  			return n, "f"
  		}
  		return n, ""
  	}
  	if n := len(s) - len(strings.TrimLeft(s, " \t\n")); n > 0 {
  		return n, ""
  	}
  	_, size := utf8.DecodeRuneInString(s)
  	return size, ""
  }
  ```

  #strings and identifiers{
    A string ends at its closing quote. A backslash skips the next character unless the string is raw. A
    string that is still open at the end of a line is closed there, except a backquoted one, which may span
    lines in Go and JavaScript; this keeps a stray apostrophe from colouring the rest of a file.

    ```
    func scanString(s string, raw bool) int {
    	q := s[0]
    	for j := 1; j < len(s); j++ {
    		switch {
    		case s[j] == '\\' && !raw:
    			j++
    		case s[j] == q:
    			return j + 1
    		case s[j] == '\n' && q != '`':
    			return j
    		}
    	}
    	return len(s)
    }

    func identLen(s string) int {
    	n := 0
    	for n < len(s) {
    		r, size := utf8.DecodeRuneInString(s[n:])
    		if r == '_' || unicode.IsLetter(r) || (n > 0 && unicode.IsDigit(r)) {
    			n += size
    		} else {
    			break
    		}
    	}
    	return n
    }
    ```
  }
}

#brashtag highlighting{
  For brashtag itself the interesting tokens are the structure: a tag with its opening brace, a closing brace,
  and code between backticks. Those are coloured as keywords and strings, and everything else is text. A `#`
  that is not followed by a brace on the same line is left alone.

  ```
  func highlightBrashtag(code string) string {
  	var b strings.Builder
  	for i := 0; i < len(code); {
  		switch c := code[i]; {
  		case c == '#':
  			if j := strings.IndexAny(code[i:], "{}`\n"); j > 0 && code[i+j] == '{' {
  				span(&b, "k", code[i:i+j+1])
  				i += j + 1
  				continue
  			}
  			b.WriteString("#")
  			i++
  		case c == '}':
  			span(&b, "k", "}")
  			i++
  		case c == '`':
  			n := 0
  			for i+n < len(code) && code[i+n] == '`' {
  				n++
  			}
  			end := strings.Index(code[i+n:], code[i:i+n])
  			if end < 0 {
  				span(&b, "s", code[i:])
  				i = len(code)
  				continue
  			}
  			span(&b, "s", code[i:i+2*n+end])
  			i += 2*n + end
  		default:
  			j := strings.IndexAny(code[i:], "#}`")
  			if j < 0 {
  				j = len(code) - i
  			}
  			b.WriteString(html.EscapeString(code[i : i+j]))
  			i += j
  		}
  	}
  	return b.String()
  }
  ```
}
#h1{The site}

The site ties documents together. It reads `site.bt`, loads every post and page, decides which of them to
publish, gives each a URL, groups posts by tag, links each post to its neighbours, and renders them all. Only
then does anything get written, in the next section, so that a `#ref` in the first post can name the last one.

#h2{Configuration}

#site configuration{
  The configuration is itself written in brashtag, one bag per setting:

  #sample site configuration{
  ````
  #title{Notes from the barkdown}
  #baseurl{https://example.com/blog/}
  #author{Ada}
  #perpage{5}
  #nav{ #link{/ Home} #link{/about/ About} #link{/archive/ Archive} }
  #footer{Written in #link{https://github.com/PratikDeoghare/brashtag brashtag}.}
  ````
  }

  Every setting has a default, so an empty `site.bt`, or none at all, still gives a working site.
  `#dateformat` is written as Go writes date layouts, by showing how the reference time, the second of
  January 2006, should look. `#permalink` is a pattern for post URLs with the placeholders `:year`, `:month`,
  `:day` and `:slug`. `#mathjax` and `#mermaid` override the script URLs, pinned to exact versions so that a
  site does not change behaviour when those libraries publish a new release. `#fonts` is the folder the theme
  loads the ET Book typeface from, the one Tufte CSS ships; an empty `#fonts{}` turns web fonts off, and the
  theme falls back to Palatino.

  ```
  // Config is the content of site.bt.
  type Config struct {
  	Title       string
  	Subtitle    string
  	Description string
  	Author      string
  	BaseURL     string
  	Language    string
  	DateFormat  string
  	PerPage     int
  	Permalink   string
  	MathJax     string
  	Mermaid     string
  	Fonts       string
  	Nav         []NavItem
  	Footer      template.HTML
  	Meta        map[string]string
  	footer      []bt.Node
  }

  type NavItem struct{ Title, URL string }

  func defaultConfig() Config {
  	return Config{
  		Title:      "My barkdown site",
  		BaseURL:    "/",
  		Language:   "en",
  		DateFormat: "2 January 2006",
  		PerPage:    10,
  		Permalink:  "/posts/:slug/",
  		MathJax:    "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3.2.2/es5/tex-chtml.js",
  		Mermaid:    "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mermaid@10.9.1/dist/mermaid.esm.min.mjs",
  		Fonts:      "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/tufte-css@1.8.0/et-book",
  		Meta:       map[string]string{},
  	}
  }
  ```

  #load the configuration{
    The footer is kept as nodes and rendered later, once the base path is known, because it may contain
    links. Navigation entries are `#link` bags, using the same first-word convention as in posts.

    ```
    // LoadConfig reads site.bt; a missing file gives the defaults.
    func LoadConfig(path string) (Config, error) {
    	c := defaultConfig()
    	src, err := os.ReadFile(path)
    	if errors.Is(err, fs.ErrNotExist) {
    		return c, nil
    	}
    	if err != nil {
    		return c, err
    	}
    	tree, err := parseBrashtag(string(src))
    	if err != nil {
    		return c, fileError(filepath.Base(path), err)
    	}
    	for _, kid := range tree.Kids() {
    		b, ok := kid.(bt.Bag)
    		if !ok {
    			continue
    		}
    		tag := strings.TrimSpace(b.Tag())
    		val := strings.Join(strings.Fields(textOf(b)), " ")
    		c.Meta[tag] = val
    		switch tag {
    		case "title":
    			c.Title = val
    		case "subtitle":
    			c.Subtitle = val
    		case "description":
    			c.Description = val
    		case "author":
    			c.Author = val
    		case "baseurl", "url":
    			c.BaseURL = val
    		case "language", "lang":
    			c.Language = val
    		case "dateformat":
    			c.DateFormat = val
    		case "permalink":
    			c.Permalink = val
    		case "mathjax":
    			c.MathJax = val
    		case "mermaid":
    			c.Mermaid = val
    		case "fonts":
    			c.Fonts = strings.TrimSuffix(val, "/")
    		case "perpage":
    			n, err := strconv.Atoi(val)
    			if err != nil || n < 1 {
    				return c, fmt.Errorf("site.bt: #perpage{%s}: need a positive number", val)
    			}
    			c.PerPage = n
    		case "nav":
    			for _, k := range b.Kids() {
    				if l, ok := k.(bt.Bag); ok && (strings.TrimSpace(l.Tag()) == "link" || strings.TrimSpace(l.Tag()) == "a") {
    					u, rest := firstWord(l.Kids())
    					c.Nav = append(c.Nav, NavItem{Title: words(rest), URL: u})
    				}
    			}
    		case "footer":
    			c.footer = b.Kids()
    		}
    	}
    	return c, nil
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{The site and its options}

#site type{
  `Options` are what the command line controls. `Serving` is set by the development server; it makes absolute
  URLs point at the local server and turns on live reloading. `Now` is a field rather than a call to
  `time.Now` so that tests can decide which posts are in the future.

  ```
  // Options control a build.
  type Options struct {
  	Src, Out string
  	Drafts   bool      // include drafts
  	Future   bool      // include posts dated in the future
  	BaseURL  string    // overrides #baseurl in site.bt
  	Serving  string    // host:port of the development server, if any
  	Now      time.Time // the time the build happens
  }

  // Site is everything known about a site while it is built.
  type Site struct {
  	Config
  	opts       Options
  	root       string
  	basePath   string // the path of BaseURL, with a slash at both ends
  	absBase    string // scheme, host and base path; "" when BaseURL is relative
  	Posts      []*Doc
  	Pages      []*Doc
  	Tags       []*Tag
  	Menu       []NavItem
  	LiveReload bool
  	Built      time.Time
  	Warnings   []string
  	Skipped    int    // drafts and future posts left out
  	Timeline   []*Doc // posts with thoughts, the most recently added to first
  	bySlug     map[string]*Doc

  	// Used while writing.
  	theme     fs.FS
  	templates map[string]*template.Template
  	assets    map[string]string
  	sitemap   []sitemapURL
  	pages     int
  }

  // Tag is a tag and the posts that carry it, newest first.
  type Tag struct {
  	Name, Slug, URL string
  	Posts           []*Doc
  }

  func (s *Site) warnf(format string, args ...any) {
  	s.Warnings = append(s.Warnings, fmt.Sprintf(format, args...))
  }

  func (s *Site) all() []*Doc {
  	return append(append([]*Doc(nil), s.Posts...), s.Pages...)
  }
  ```
}

#base urls{
  A site may live at the root of a domain or below it, for example on GitHub Pages at
  `https://user.github.io/blog/`. Every link barkdown writes is therefore an absolute path that starts with the
  base path, `/blog/`, and the few places that need a full URL (feeds, the sitemap, canonical links, Open Graph
  tags) put the scheme and host in front. If `#baseurl` has no host, the site still works, and the feeds fall
  back to paths with a warning, because feed readers need absolute URLs.

  ```
  func (s *Site) setBase() error {
  	u, err := url.Parse(s.BaseURL)
  	if err != nil {
  		return fmt.Errorf("site.bt: #baseurl{%s}: %v", s.BaseURL, err)
  	}
  	p := u.Path
  	if !strings.HasPrefix(p, "/") {
  		p = "/" + p
  	}
  	if !strings.HasSuffix(p, "/") {
  		p += "/"
  	}
  	s.basePath = p
  	if u.Scheme != "" && u.Host != "" {
  		s.absBase = u.Scheme + "://" + u.Host + p
  	}
  	if s.opts.Serving != "" {
  		s.absBase = "http://" + s.opts.Serving + p
  	}
  	return nil
  }

  // URL turns a site path such as "/posts/x/" into a link with the base path.
  func (s *Site) URL(p string) string {
  	if isExternal(p) || !strings.HasPrefix(p, "/") {
  		return p
  	}
  	return s.basePath + strings.TrimPrefix(p, "/")
  }

  // Abs turns a site path into an absolute URL.
  func (s *Site) Abs(p string) string {
  	if isExternal(p) || s.absBase == "" {
  		return s.URL(p)
  	}
  	return s.absBase + strings.TrimPrefix(p, "/")
  }
  ```
}

#h2{Loading}

#load a site{
  `Load` is the whole first half of a build, step by step.

  ```
  // Load reads and renders a site without writing anything.
  func Load(opts Options) (*Site, error) {
  	if opts.Now.IsZero() {
  		opts.Now = time.Now()
  	}
  	cfg, err := LoadConfig(filepath.Join(opts.Src, "site.bt"))
  	if err != nil {
  		return nil, err
  	}
  	if opts.BaseURL != "" {
  		cfg.BaseURL = opts.BaseURL
  	}
  	s := &Site{Config: cfg, opts: opts, root: opts.Src, bySlug: map[string]*Doc{},
  		Built: opts.Now, LiveReload: opts.Serving != ""}
  	if err := s.setBase(); err != nil {
  		return nil, err
  	}
  	<<<load the documents>>>
  	<<<sort and give urls>>>
  	<<<collect tags>>>
  	<<<link neighbours>>>
  	<<<render everything>>>
  	<<<build the menu>>>
  	return s, nil
  }
  ```
}

#load the documents{
  Posts live under `posts`, pages under `pages`. Both are walked recursively, so posts can be filed in
  folders by year or topic. Every file is parsed even after an error, and all errors are reported together,
  which saves a round trip per mistake. Drafts and posts dated in the future are then left out, unless asked
  for.

  ```
  posts, err := s.loadDir("posts", "post")
  if err != nil {
  	return nil, err
  }
  pages, err := s.loadDir("pages", "page")
  if err != nil {
  	return nil, err
  }
  for _, d := range append(posts, pages...) {
  	switch {
  	case d.Draft && !opts.Drafts, d.Kind == "post" && d.Date.After(opts.Now) && !opts.Future:
  		s.Skipped++
  	case d.Kind == "post":
  		s.Posts = append(s.Posts, d)
  	default:
  		s.Pages = append(s.Pages, d)
  	}
  }
  ```

  #walk a content directory{
    Directories whose names start with a dot, such as an editor's swap folder, are skipped.

    ```
    func (s *Site) loadDir(dir, kind string) ([]*Doc, error) {
    	root := filepath.Join(s.root, dir)
    	if _, err := os.Stat(root); errors.Is(err, fs.ErrNotExist) {
    		return nil, nil
    	}
    	var docs []*Doc
    	var errs []error
    	err := filepath.WalkDir(root, func(path string, d fs.DirEntry, err error) error {
    		if err != nil {
    			return err
    		}
    		if d.IsDir() {
    			if path != root && strings.HasPrefix(d.Name(), ".") {
    				return filepath.SkipDir
    			}
    			return nil
    		}
    		if filepath.Ext(path) != ".bt" {
    			return nil
    		}
    		doc, err := s.loadDoc(path, root, kind)
    		if err != nil {
    			errs = append(errs, err)
    		} else {
    			docs = append(docs, doc)
    		}
    		return nil
    	})
    	if err != nil {
    		return nil, err
    	}
    	return docs, errors.Join(errs...)
    }
    ```
  }

  #load one document{
    Here the file name fills in what the document did not say: its slug, its date, and as a last resort its
    title. A post with no date at all gets its file's modification time and a warning, since that time changes
    whenever the file is copied. A file named `index.bt` in a folder of its own is a page bundle.

    ```
    func (s *Site) loadDoc(path, root, kind string) (*Doc, error) {
    	src, err := os.ReadFile(path)
    	if err != nil {
    		return nil, err
    	}
    	rel, _ := filepath.Rel(s.root, path)
    	d, err := ParseDoc(filepath.ToSlash(rel), string(src))
    	if err != nil {
    		return nil, err
    	}
    	d.Kind = kind
    	slug, date := nameParts(path)
    	if d.Slug == "" {
    		d.Slug = slug
    	}
    	if d.Date.IsZero() {
    		d.Date = date
    	}
    	if filepath.Base(path) == "index.bt" && filepath.Dir(path) != root {
    		d.bundle = filepath.Dir(path)
    	}
    	if d.Title == "" {
    		d.Title = humanize(d.Slug)
    		s.warnf("%s: no #title{}, using %q", d.Source, d.Title)
    	}
    	if kind == "post" && d.Date.IsZero() {
    		if info, err := os.Stat(path); err == nil {
    			d.Date = info.ModTime()
    		}
    		s.warnf("%s: no #date{} and no date in the file name; using the file's modification time", d.Source)
    	}
    	return d, nil
    }
    ```
  }
}

#sort and give urls{
  Posts are listed newest first, with the title breaking ties so that the order never depends on the order
  of the file system. Pages are ordered by `#weight`, then title.

  Slugs must be unique across posts and pages, because `#ref` looks them up in one table, and URLs must be
  unique, which a permalink pattern without `:slug` could break. Pages may not take the names of the
  directories barkdown generates.

  ```
  sort.SliceStable(s.Posts, func(i, j int) bool {
  	a, b := s.Posts[i], s.Posts[j]
  	if !a.Date.Equal(b.Date) {
  		return a.Date.After(b.Date)
  	}
  	return a.Title < b.Title
  })
  sort.SliceStable(s.Pages, func(i, j int) bool {
  	a, b := s.Pages[i], s.Pages[j]
  	if a.Weight != b.Weight {
  		return a.Weight < b.Weight
  	}
  	return a.Title < b.Title
  })
  reserved := map[string]bool{"tags": true, "archive": true, "search": true, "page": true, "assets": true, "timeline": true}
  urls := map[string]*Doc{}
  for _, d := range s.all() {
  	if d.Kind == "post" {
  		d.URL = s.permalink(d)
  	} else {
  		d.URL = "/" + d.Slug + "/"
  		if reserved[d.Slug] || d.Slug == "" {
  			return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: a page cannot be called %q; set another #slug{}", d.Source, d.Slug)
  		}
  	}
  	if other := s.bySlug[d.Slug]; other != nil {
  		return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s and %s have the same slug %q; set #slug{} in one of them", other.Source, d.Source, d.Slug)
  	}
  	if other := urls[d.URL]; other != nil {
  		return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s and %s would both be published at %s", other.Source, d.Source, d.URL)
  	}
  	s.bySlug[d.Slug], urls[d.URL] = d, d
  }
  ```

  #permalinks{
    A pattern that ends in a file name such as `.html` is kept as a file; otherwise the URL is a directory,
    and its page is written as `index.html` inside it, which every static host serves without the file name.

    ```
    func (s *Site) permalink(d *Doc) string {
    	p := strings.NewReplacer(
    		":year", d.Date.Format("2006"), ":month", d.Date.Format("01"),
    		":day", d.Date.Format("02"), ":slug", d.Slug,
    	).Replace(s.Permalink)
    	if !strings.HasPrefix(p, "/") {
    		p = "/" + p
    	}
    	if !strings.HasSuffix(p, "/") && path.Ext(p) == "" {
    		p += "/"
    	}
    	return p
    }
    ```
  }
}

#collect tags{
  Tags are matched by slug, so `Go` and `go` are one tag; the first spelling seen names it. Because posts are
  already sorted, every tag's posts are too.

  ```
  byTag := map[string]*Tag{}
  for _, d := range s.Posts {
  	for _, name := range d.Tags {
  		slug := slugify(name)
  		if slug == "" {
  			continue
  		}
  		t := byTag[slug]
  		if t == nil {
  			t = &Tag{Name: name, Slug: slug, URL: "/tags/" + slug + "/"}
  			byTag[slug] = t
  			s.Tags = append(s.Tags, t)
  		}
  		t.Posts = append(t.Posts, d)
  		d.TagRefs = append(d.TagRefs, t)
  	}
  }
  sort.Slice(s.Tags, func(i, j int) bool {
  	return strings.ToLower(s.Tags[i].Name) < strings.ToLower(s.Tags[j].Name)
  })
  ```
}

#link neighbours{
  `Prev` is the older post and `Next` the newer one. Related posts are those sharing the most tags; the sort
  is stable, so among equally related posts the newest come first.

  ```
  for i, d := range s.Posts {
  	if i > 0 {
  		d.Next = s.Posts[i-1]
  	}
  	if i+1 < len(s.Posts) {
  		d.Prev = s.Posts[i+1]
  	}
  	d.Related = s.related(d, 3)
  }
  ```

  #related posts{
    ```
    func (s *Site) related(d *Doc, n int) []*Doc {
    	type scored struct {
    		doc   *Doc
    		score int
    	}
    	var cands []scored
    	for _, o := range s.Posts {
    		if o == d {
    			continue
    		}
    		score := 0
    		for _, t := range d.TagRefs {
    			for _, u := range o.TagRefs {
    				if t == u {
    					score++
    				}
    			}
    		}
    		if score > 0 {
    			cands = append(cands, scored{o, score})
    		}
    	}
    	sort.SliceStable(cands, func(i, j int) bool { return cands[i].score > cands[j].score })
    	var out []*Doc
    	for _, c := range cands[:min(n, len(cands))] {
    		out = append(out, c.doc)
    	}
    	return out
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Rendering every document}

#render everything{
  ```
  for _, d := range s.all() {
  	s.render(d)
  }
  if s.footer != nil {
  	s.Footer = template.HTML(NewRenderer(s).compactFlow(s.footer))
  }
  <<<collect the timeline>>>
  ```
}

#render one document{
  Besides the HTML, rendering produces what lists and feeds show about a document. The description, used in
  `<meta>` tags and feeds, is the `#summary` or else the first thirty words. Reading time assumes 238 words a
  minute, the average silent reading rate of English non-fiction found by a meta-analysis of 190 studies
  #cite{brysbaert2019}; the more familiar figures of 250 or 300 are higher than most readers manage.

  ```
  const wordsPerMinute = 238

  func (s *Site) render(d *Doc) {
  	r := NewRenderer(s)
  	r.newest = d.Meta["order"] == "newest"
  	d.Content = template.HTML(r.Render(d.body))
  	d.Math, d.Mermaid = r.math, r.mermaid
  	<<<note the latest thought>>>
  	for _, w := range r.Warnings {
  		s.warnf("%s: %s", d.Source, w)
  	}
  	d.Text = plainText(d.body)
  	d.Words = len(strings.Fields(d.Text))
  	d.ReadingTime = max(1, int(math.Round(float64(d.Words)/wordsPerMinute)))
  	d.Description = d.Summary
  	if d.Description == "" {
  		d.Description = truncateWords(d.Text, 30)
  	}
  	<<<choose the excerpt>>>
  }
  ```

  #note the latest thought{
    A post that grows by thoughts changes whenever one is added, and its date of last change should say so:
    feed readers then show it again, and search engines see a fresh `lastmod` in the sitemap. So the latest
    thought becomes the post's `Updated` time, unless `#updated` already names a later one.

    ```
    d.Thoughts = r.thoughts
    if t := d.Latest(); t != nil {
    	d.LastThought = t.Time
    	if t.Time.After(d.Updated) && t.Time.After(d.Date) {
    		d.Updated = t.Time
    	}
    }
    ```
  }

  #choose the excerpt{
    The excerpt shown on list pages is, in order of preference: everything before a `#more{}` bag, rendered
    as HTML; the `#summary`; or the first forty words. Only the first keeps formatting, which is why `#more`
    exists. The excerpt remembers whether it needs MathJax or Mermaid itself, so that a list page loads them
    only when an excerpt on it does. Its renderer runs in excerpt mode, without footnotes or a table of contents, and its warnings are
    dropped, since the full render has reported them already.

    ```
    if i := moreIndex(d.body); i >= 0 {
    	er := NewRenderer(s)
    	er.excerpt = true
    	d.Excerpt = template.HTML(er.Render(d.body[:i]))
    	d.HasMore = true
    	d.excerptMath, d.excerptMermaid = er.math, er.mermaid
    } else if d.Summary != "" {
    	d.Excerpt = template.HTML("<p>" + html.EscapeString(d.Summary) + "</p>")
    } else {
    	d.Excerpt = template.HTML("<p>" + html.EscapeString(truncateWords(d.Text, 40)) + "</p>")
    }
    ```

    #find the more marker{
      ```
      func moreIndex(nodes []bt.Node) int {
      	for i, n := range nodes {
      		if b, ok := n.(bt.Bag); ok && strings.TrimSpace(b.Tag()) == "more" {
      			return i
      		}
      	}
      	return -1
      }
      ```
    }
  }
}

#collect the timeline{
  The timeline page lists the posts that have thoughts, the one with the most recent thought first, so a post
  that just grew floats to the top however old it is.

  ```
  for _, d := range s.Posts {
  	if len(d.Thoughts) > 0 {
  		s.Timeline = append(s.Timeline, d)
  	}
  }
  sort.SliceStable(s.Timeline, func(i, j int) bool { return s.Timeline[i].LastThought.After(s.Timeline[j].LastThought) })
  ```
}

#build the menu{
  Without a `#nav`, the menu is made from the pages, ordered by weight, with the timeline first when some posts
  have thoughts; a page with a negative weight is published but left out of the menu.

  ```
  if len(s.Nav) > 0 {
  	s.Menu = s.Nav
  } else {
  	s.Menu = []NavItem{{"Home", "/"}}
  	if len(s.Timeline) > 0 {
  		s.Menu = append(s.Menu, NavItem{"Timeline", "/timeline/"})
  	}
  	for _, p := range s.Pages {
  		if p.Weight >= 0 {
  			s.Menu = append(s.Menu, NavItem{p.Title, p.URL})
  		}
  	}
  	s.Menu = append(s.Menu, NavItem{"Archive", "/archive/"}, NavItem{"Tags", "/tags/"}, NavItem{"Search", "/search/"})
  }
  ```
}
#h1{Writing the site}

The second half of a build writes files. Each step is a method on the site, and they run in a fixed order:
assets first, because pages refer to them by a hash of their content; then the pages, feeds, sitemap and search
index; then the files copied from page bundles and `static`, last so that a file in `static` can replace anything
barkdown generated. Finally every page is read back and its links are checked.

#write the site{
  ```
  // Write generates the site into the output directory.
  func (s *Site) Write() error {
  	if err := prepareOut(s.opts.Out); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	s.loadTheme()
  	steps := []func() error{
  		s.writeAssets, s.writeHTML, s.writeFeeds, s.writeSitemap,
  		s.writeSearchIndex, s.copyBundles, s.copyStatic,
  	}
  	for _, step := range steps {
  		if err := step(); err != nil {
  			return err
  		}
  	}
  	s.checkLinks()
  	return nil
  }

  // Build loads a site and writes it.
  func Build(opts Options) (*Site, error) {
  	s, err := Load(opts)
  	if err != nil {
  		return nil, err
  	}
  	return s, s.Write()
  }
  ```
}

#h2{A safe output directory}

#prepare the output directory{
  Every build starts from an empty directory, so that a deleted post does not linger on the server. Emptying a
  directory is dangerous when someone passes the wrong one, say `-out .` in their home folder. So barkdown leaves
  a marker file `.barkdown` in every directory it builds and refuses to empty a non-empty directory without one.
  It also writes `.nojekyll`, which tells GitHub Pages to serve the files as they are instead of running Jekyll
  over them.

  ```
  const marker = ".barkdown"

  // prepareOut empties the output directory, but only one that barkdown made.
  func prepareOut(out string) error {
  	entries, err := os.ReadDir(out)
  	if err != nil && !errors.Is(err, fs.ErrNotExist) {
  		return err
  	}
  	if len(entries) > 0 {
  		if _, err := os.Stat(filepath.Join(out, marker)); err != nil {
  			return fmt.Errorf("refusing to empty %s: it is not empty and barkdown did not make it (no %s file)", out, marker)
  		}
  		for _, e := range entries {
  			if err := os.RemoveAll(filepath.Join(out, e.Name())); err != nil {
  				return err
  			}
  		}
  	}
  	if err := os.MkdirAll(out, 0o755); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	if err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(out, ".nojekyll"), nil, 0o644); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	return os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(out, marker), []byte("Generated by barkdown; emptied on every build.\n"), 0o644)
  }

  // writeFile writes data at a site path; a path ending in "/" gets index.html.
  func (s *Site) writeFile(p string, data []byte) error {
  	if strings.HasSuffix(p, "/") {
  		p += "index.html"
  	}
  	file := filepath.Join(s.opts.Out, filepath.FromSlash(strings.TrimPrefix(p, "/")))
  	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(file), 0o755); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	return os.WriteFile(file, data, 0o644)
  }
  ```
}

#h2{Theme and templates}

#load the theme{
  The built-in theme is compiled into the binary with `go:embed`, so a barkdown binary is the whole program. A
  site can replace any theme file by putting a file of the same name in its `templates` directory, from a
  single template to the stylesheet. The two are combined by a tiny overlay file system that looks in the site's
  directory first.

  ```
  //go:embed theme
  var themeFS embed.FS

  // overlayFS serves a file from top when it exists there, else from bottom.
  type overlayFS struct{ top, bottom fs.FS }

  func (o overlayFS) Open(name string) (fs.File, error) {
  	if f, err := o.top.Open(name); err == nil {
  		return f, nil
  	}
  	return o.bottom.Open(name)
  }

  func (s *Site) loadTheme() {
  	builtin, _ := fs.Sub(themeFS, "theme")
  	s.theme = overlayFS{os.DirFS(filepath.Join(s.root, "templates")), builtin}
  	s.templates = map[string]*template.Template{}
  }
  ```

  #templates{
    Each kind of page is a template file that defines `content`, parsed together with `base.html`, which holds
    the surrounding layout, and `partials.html`, which holds pieces shared between pages. A document's `#layout`
    names another file of the same kind, so one post can look different from the rest. Templates are parsed when
    first needed and kept.

    The functions available in templates are few: `url` and `abs` from the site, `asset` for theme files with
    their content hash, and three ways to format a date.

    ```
    func (s *Site) template(name string) (*template.Template, error) {
    	if t, ok := s.templates[name]; ok {
    		return t, nil
    	}
    	if strings.ContainsAny(name, `/\`) {
    		return nil, fmt.Errorf("layout %q: a layout is a file name in templates/, without directories", name)
    	}
    	t, err := template.New("base.html").Funcs(s.funcs()).ParseFS(s.theme, "base.html", "partials.html", name+".html")
    	if err != nil {
    		return nil, fmt.Errorf("template %s: %v", name, err)
    	}
    	s.templates[name] = t
    	return t, nil
    }

    func (s *Site) funcs() template.FuncMap {
    	return template.FuncMap{
    		"url": s.URL,
    		"abs": s.Abs,
    		"asset": func(name string) string {
    			if u, ok := s.assets[name]; ok {
    				return u
    			}
    			return s.URL("/assets/" + name)
    		},
    		"date":    func(t time.Time) string { return t.Format(s.DateFormat) },
    		"iso":     func(t time.Time) string { return t.Format("2006-01-02") },
    		"rfc3339": func(t time.Time) string { return t.Format(time.RFC3339) },
    		"now":     func() time.Time { return s.Built },
    		"stamp":   func(t *Thought) string { return formatStamp(t, s.DateFormat) },
    	}
    }
    ```
  }

  #theme assets{
    The stylesheet and script are linked with `?v=` and the first eight hexadecimal digits of their SHA-256.
    A host may tell browsers to cache them for a long time; when the file changes, so does its URL, and readers
    get the new version at once.

    ```
    var themeAssets = []string{"style.css", "site.js", "favicon.svg"}

    func (s *Site) writeAssets() error {
    	s.assets = map[string]string{}
    	for _, name := range themeAssets {
    		data, err := fs.ReadFile(s.theme, name)
    		if err != nil {
    			return err
    		}
    		target := "/assets/" + name
    		if name == "favicon.svg" {
    			target = "/favicon.svg"
    		}
    		if err := s.writeFile(target, data); err != nil {
    			return err
    		}
    		sum := sha256.Sum256(data)
    		s.assets[name] = s.URL(target) + "?v=" + hex.EncodeToString(sum[:4])
    	}
    	return nil
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Pages}

#page data{
  A template sees a `Page`. Only the fields that make sense for its kind are set: a post page has `Doc`, a list
  has `Posts` and perhaps a `Pager` or a `Tag`, the archive has `Years`. `Math` and `Mermaid` tell the layout
  which scripts to load.

  ```
  // Page is what a template sees.
  type Page struct {
  	Site          *Site
  	Kind          string // home, post, page, tag, tags, archive, search or 404
  	Title         string
  	Description   string
  	URL           string
  	Doc           *Doc
  	Posts         []*Doc
  	Tag           *Tag
  	Pager         *Pager
  	Years         []Year
  	Math, Mermaid bool
  }

  // Pager links a page of a list to its neighbours.
  type Pager struct {
  	Number, Total int
  	Newer, Older  string
  }

  // Year is one year of the archive.
  type Year struct {
  	Year  int
  	Posts []*Doc
  }
  ```

  #render a page{
    Rendering a page into a buffer first means a template error never leaves half a file behind. Every page
    except the search page and the 404 page is remembered for the sitemap.

    ```
    func (s *Site) page(tmpl string, p *Page) error {
    	t, err := s.template(tmpl)
    	if err != nil {
    		return err
    	}
    	p.Site = s
    	if p.Description == "" {
    		p.Description = s.Description
    	}
    	var buf bytes.Buffer
    	if err := t.ExecuteTemplate(&buf, "base", p); err != nil {
    		return fmt.Errorf("%s: %v", p.URL, err)
    	}
    	if p.Kind != "search" && p.Kind != "404" {
    		entry := sitemapURL{Loc: s.Abs(p.URL)}
    		if p.Doc != nil && p.Doc.Kind == "post" {
    			entry.LastMod = lastModified(p.Doc).Format("2006-01-02")
    		}
    		s.sitemap = append(s.sitemap, entry)
    	}
    	s.pages++
    	return s.writeFile(p.URL, buf.Bytes())
    }

    func lastModified(d *Doc) time.Time {
    	if d.Updated.After(d.Date) {
    		return d.Updated
    	}
    	return d.Date
    }

    // anyMath says whether the excerpts of docs need MathJax or Mermaid.
    func anyMath(docs []*Doc) (bool, bool) {
    	var m, d bool
    	for _, doc := range docs {
    		m, d = m || doc.excerptMath, d || doc.excerptMermaid
    	}
    	return m, d
    }
    ```
  }
}

#write the pages{
  ```
  func (s *Site) writeHTML() error {
  	<<<write the home pages>>>
  	<<<write posts and pages>>>
  	<<<write the tag pages>>>
  	<<<write the archive, search and 404 pages>>>
  	return nil
  }
  ```
}

#write the home pages{
  The home page lists the newest posts, `#perpage` at a time; older ones continue at `/page/2/` and so on.
  A site without posts still gets a home page.

  ```
  per := s.PerPage
  total := max(1, (len(s.Posts)+per-1)/per)
  for n := 1; n <= total; n++ {
  	posts := s.Posts[(n-1)*per : min(n*per, len(s.Posts))]
  	p := &Page{Kind: "home", URL: pageURL(n), Posts: posts, Pager: &Pager{Number: n, Total: total}}
  	p.Math, p.Mermaid = anyMath(posts)
  	if n > 1 {
  		p.Title = fmt.Sprintf("Page %d", n)
  		p.Pager.Newer = pageURL(n - 1)
  	}
  	if n < total {
  		p.Pager.Older = pageURL(n + 1)
  	}
  	if err := s.page("list", p); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  }
  ```

  #page urls{
    ```
    func pageURL(n int) string {
    	if n == 1 {
    		return "/"
    	}
    	return fmt.Sprintf("/page/%d/", n)
    }
    ```
  }
}

#write posts and pages{
  ```
  for _, d := range s.all() {
  	layout := d.Layout
  	if layout == "" {
  		layout = d.Kind
  	}
  	p := &Page{Kind: d.Kind, Title: d.Title, Description: d.Description, URL: d.URL, Doc: d,
  		Math: d.Math, Mermaid: d.Mermaid}
  	if err := s.page(layout, p); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  }
  ```
}

#write the tag pages{
  ```
  for _, t := range s.Tags {
  	p := &Page{Kind: "tag", Title: "Filed under " + t.Name, URL: t.URL, Posts: t.Posts, Tag: t,
  		Description: fmt.Sprintf("Posts tagged %s", t.Name)}
  	p.Math, p.Mermaid = anyMath(t.Posts)
  	if err := s.page("list", p); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  }
  if err := s.page("tags", &Page{Kind: "tags", Title: "Tags", URL: "/tags/"}); err != nil {
  	return err
  }
  ```
}

#write the archive, search and 404 pages{
  The archive groups posts by year; since posts are sorted, a new group starts whenever the year changes. The
  404 page is written as `404.html` at the top of the site, where GitHub Pages, Netlify and Cloudflare Pages
  look for it. Its links work from any URL because every link barkdown writes is an absolute path.

  ```
  var years []Year
  for _, d := range s.Posts {
  	if y := d.Date.Year(); len(years) == 0 || years[len(years)-1].Year != y {
  		years = append(years, Year{Year: y})
  	}
  	years[len(years)-1].Posts = append(years[len(years)-1].Posts, d)
  }
  for _, p := range []*Page{
  	{Kind: "archive", Title: "Archive", URL: "/archive/", Years: years},
  	{Kind: "search", Title: "Search", URL: "/search/"},
  	{Kind: "timeline", Title: "Timeline", URL: "/timeline/", Posts: s.Timeline},
  	{Kind: "404", Title: "Page not found", URL: "/404.html"},
  } {
  	if err := s.page(p.Kind, p); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  }
  ```
}

#h2{Feeds}

#write the feeds{
  Barkdown writes both an Atom feed, `feed.xml`, and an RSS 2.0 feed, `rss.xml`, of the newest twenty posts with
  their full content. Atom is the better specified of the two #cite{rfc4287}; RSS is what some readers and most
  podcast tools still look for #cite{rss2}. Both are generated from Go structs with `encoding/xml`, which takes
  care of escaping.

  ```
  const feedSize = 20

  func (s *Site) writeFeeds() error {
  	if s.absBase == "" && len(s.Posts) > 0 {
  		s.warnf("site.bt: #baseurl{%s} has no scheme and host; feeds and the sitemap will contain relative links", s.BaseURL)
  	}
  	posts := s.Posts[:min(feedSize, len(s.Posts))]
  	updated := s.Built
  	if len(posts) > 0 {
  		updated = lastModified(posts[0])
  		for _, d := range posts {
  			if t := lastModified(d); t.After(updated) {
  				updated = t
  			}
  		}
  	}
  	<<<write the atom feed>>>
  	<<<write the rss feed>>>
  	if err := s.writeThoughtFeed(); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	return nil
  }

  func (s *Site) writeXML(p string, v any) error {
  	data, err := xml.MarshalIndent(v, "", "  ")
  	if err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	return s.writeFile(p, append([]byte(xml.Header), append(data, '\n')...))
  }
  ```
}

#atom feed types{
  Atom requires an `id`, a `title` and an `updated` time for the feed and for every entry, and an author for
  every entry, given either there or on the feed. The content is HTML, so its links are site paths; the
  `xml:base` attribute gives the post's own URL, against which feed readers resolve relative references,
  which is what makes the images of a page bundle appear in a reader.

  ```
  type atomFeed struct {
  	XMLName   xml.Name    `xml:"http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom feed"`
  	Title     string      `xml:"title"`
  	Subtitle  string      `xml:"subtitle,omitempty"`
  	ID        string      `xml:"id"`
  	Updated   string      `xml:"updated"`
  	Links     []atomLink  `xml:"link"`
  	Author    atomPerson  `xml:"author"`
  	Generator string      `xml:"generator"`
  	Entries   []atomEntry `xml:"entry"`
  }

  type atomLink struct {
  	Href string `xml:"href,attr"`
  	Rel  string `xml:"rel,attr,omitempty"`
  	Type string `xml:"type,attr,omitempty"`
  }

  type atomPerson struct {
  	Name string `xml:"name"`
  }

  type atomText struct {
  	Type string `xml:"type,attr"`
  	Base string `xml:"xml:base,attr,omitempty"`
  	Body string `xml:",chardata"`
  }

  type atomCategory struct {
  	Term string `xml:"term,attr"`
  }

  type atomEntry struct {
  	Title      string         `xml:"title"`
  	ID         string         `xml:"id"`
  	Published  string         `xml:"published"`
  	Updated    string         `xml:"updated"`
  	Link       atomLink       `xml:"link"`
  	Author     *atomPerson    `xml:"author,omitempty"`
  	Categories []atomCategory `xml:"category"`
  	Summary    atomText       `xml:"summary"`
  	Content    atomText       `xml:"content"`
  }
  ```
}

#write the atom feed{
  ```
  author := s.Author
  if author == "" {
  	author = s.Title
  }
  feed := atomFeed{
  	Title: s.Title, Subtitle: s.Subtitle, ID: s.Abs("/"), Updated: updated.Format(time.RFC3339),
  	Links: []atomLink{
  		{Href: s.Abs("/feed.xml"), Rel: "self", Type: "application/atom+xml"},
  		{Href: s.Abs("/"), Rel: "alternate", Type: "text/html"},
  	},
  	Author: atomPerson{author}, Generator: "barkdown",
  }
  for _, d := range posts {
  	e := atomEntry{
  		Title: d.Title, ID: s.Abs(d.URL),
  		Published: d.Date.Format(time.RFC3339), Updated: lastModified(d).Format(time.RFC3339),
  		Link:    atomLink{Href: s.Abs(d.URL), Rel: "alternate", Type: "text/html"},
  		Summary: atomText{Type: "text", Body: d.Description},
  		Content: atomText{Type: "html", Base: s.Abs(d.URL), Body: string(d.Content)},
  	}
  	if d.Author != "" {
  		e.Author = &atomPerson{d.Author}
  	}
  	for _, t := range d.TagRefs {
  		e.Categories = append(e.Categories, atomCategory{t.Name})
  	}
  	feed.Entries = append(feed.Entries, e)
  }
  if err := s.writeXML("/feed.xml", feed); err != nil {
  	return err
  }
  ```
}

#rss feed types{
  RSS dates follow RFC 822, which Go calls `time.RFC1123Z`. The `atom:link` with `rel="self"` is not part of
  RSS proper, but feed validators recommend it, and it needs the Atom namespace declared on the root.

  ```
  type rssFeed struct {
  	XMLName xml.Name   `xml:"rss"`
  	Version string     `xml:"version,attr"`
  	AtomNS  string     `xml:"xmlns:atom,attr"`
  	Channel rssChannel `xml:"channel"`
  }

  type rssChannel struct {
  	Title         string    `xml:"title"`
  	Link          string    `xml:"link"`
  	Description   string    `xml:"description"`
  	Language      string    `xml:"language,omitempty"`
  	LastBuildDate string    `xml:"lastBuildDate"`
  	Generator     string    `xml:"generator"`
  	Self          atomLink  `xml:"atom:link"`
  	Items         []rssItem `xml:"item"`
  }

  type rssGUID struct {
  	IsPermaLink string `xml:"isPermaLink,attr"`
  	Value       string `xml:",chardata"`
  }

  type rssItem struct {
  	Title       string   `xml:"title"`
  	Link        string   `xml:"link"`
  	GUID        rssGUID  `xml:"guid"`
  	PubDate     string   `xml:"pubDate"`
  	Categories  []string `xml:"category"`
  	Description string   `xml:"description"`
  }
  ```
}

#write the rss feed{
  ```
  description := s.Description
  if description == "" {
  	description = s.Title
  }
  rss := rssFeed{Version: "2.0", AtomNS: "http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom", Channel: rssChannel{
  	Title: s.Title, Link: s.Abs("/"), Description: description, Language: s.Language,
  	LastBuildDate: updated.Format(time.RFC1123Z), Generator: "barkdown",
  	Self: atomLink{Href: s.Abs("/rss.xml"), Rel: "self", Type: "application/rss+xml"},
  }}
  for _, d := range posts {
  	item := rssItem{Title: d.Title, Link: s.Abs(d.URL), GUID: rssGUID{"true", s.Abs(d.URL)},
  		PubDate: d.Date.Format(time.RFC1123Z), Description: string(d.Content)}
  	for _, t := range d.TagRefs {
  		item.Categories = append(item.Categories, t.Name)
  	}
  	rss.Channel.Items = append(rss.Channel.Items, item)
  }
  if err := s.writeXML("/rss.xml", rss); err != nil {
  	return err
  }
  ```
}

#thought feed{
  People who follow a growing post want to hear about each new thought, not just that the post changed. So
  there is a second Atom feed, `timeline/feed.xml`, whose entries are the fifty most recent thoughts of all
  posts. Each entry's identity is the post's URL with the thought's anchor, which does not change when later
  thoughts are added, so a feed reader shows every thought once.

  ```
  func (s *Site) writeThoughtFeed() error {
  	type entry struct {
  		d *Doc
  		t *Thought
  	}
  	var all []entry
  	for _, d := range s.Posts {
  		for _, t := range d.Thoughts {
  			if !t.Time.IsZero() {
  				all = append(all, entry{d, t})
  			}
  		}
  	}
  	sort.SliceStable(all, func(i, j int) bool { return all[i].t.Time.After(all[j].t.Time) })
  	all = all[:min(50, len(all))]
  	updated := s.Built
  	if len(all) > 0 {
  		updated = all[0].t.Time
  	}
  	author := s.Author
  	if author == "" {
  		author = s.Title
  	}
  	feed := atomFeed{
  		Title: s.Title + ": thoughts", ID: s.Abs("/timeline/"), Updated: updated.Format(time.RFC3339),
  		Links: []atomLink{
  			{Href: s.Abs("/timeline/feed.xml"), Rel: "self", Type: "application/atom+xml"},
  			{Href: s.Abs("/timeline/"), Rel: "alternate", Type: "text/html"},
  		},
  		Author: atomPerson{author}, Generator: "barkdown",
  	}
  	for _, e := range all {
  		u := s.Abs(e.d.URL) + "#" + e.t.ID
  		when := e.t.Time.Format(time.RFC3339)
  		feed.Entries = append(feed.Entries, atomEntry{
  			Title: e.d.Title + ", " + formatStamp(e.t, s.DateFormat), ID: u, Published: when, Updated: when,
  			Link:    atomLink{Href: u, Rel: "alternate", Type: "text/html"},
  			Summary: atomText{Type: "text", Body: truncateWords(e.t.Text, 30)},
  			Content: atomText{Type: "html", Base: s.Abs(e.d.URL), Body: string(e.t.HTML)},
  		})
  	}
  	return s.writeXML("/timeline/feed.xml", feed)
  }
  ```
}

#h2{Sitemap and search index}

#write the sitemap{
  The sitemap lists every page that a search engine should know about, in the format of the Sitemaps protocol
  #cite{sitemaps}, where only `loc` is required and `lastmod` is given for posts. `robots.txt` points to it.

  ```
  type urlset struct {
  	XMLName xml.Name     `xml:"http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9 urlset"`
  	URLs    []sitemapURL `xml:"url"`
  }

  type sitemapURL struct {
  	Loc     string `xml:"loc"`
  	LastMod string `xml:"lastmod,omitempty"`
  }

  func (s *Site) writeSitemap() error {
  	if err := s.writeXML("/sitemap.xml", urlset{URLs: s.sitemap}); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	robots := "User-agent: *\nAllow: /\n\nSitemap: " + s.Abs("/sitemap.xml") + "\n"
  	return s.writeFile("/robots.txt", []byte(robots))
  }
  ```
}

#write the search index{
  Search runs in the reader's browser over `search.json`: every post and page with its title, URL, date, tags,
  summary and text. Text beyond three thousand words is cut off; a match that deep in a post is rarely what
  someone searched for, and it keeps the index small enough to download on a phone.

  ```
  type searchEntry struct {
  	Title   string   `json:"title"`
  	URL     string   `json:"url"`
  	Date    string   `json:"date,omitempty"`
  	Tags    []string `json:"tags,omitempty"`
  	Summary string   `json:"summary"`
  	Text    string   `json:"text"`
  }

  func (s *Site) writeSearchIndex() error {
  	entries := []searchEntry{}
  	for _, d := range s.all() {
  		e := searchEntry{Title: d.Title, URL: s.URL(d.URL), Tags: d.Tags, Summary: d.Description,
  			Text: truncateWords(d.Text, 3000)}
  		if d.Kind == "post" {
  			e.Date = d.Date.Format(s.DateFormat)
  		}
  		entries = append(entries, e)
  	}
  	data, err := json.Marshal(entries)
  	if err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	return s.writeFile("/search.json", data)
  }
  ```
}

#h2{Copied files}

#copy bundles and static files{
  A page bundle's other files are copied next to its page, so `#img{photo.jpg}` in `posts/trip/index.bt` finds
  `posts/trip/photo.jpg`. Everything in `static` is copied to the top of the site, which is the place for a
  `CNAME` file, extra images, or a replacement `favicon.svg`. Version control directories and the macOS
  `.DS_Store` files are skipped; other dot files are kept, because `.well-known` must be published.

  ```
  func (s *Site) copyBundles() error {
  	for _, d := range s.all() {
  		if d.bundle == "" {
  			continue
  		}
  		dest := filepath.Join(s.opts.Out, filepath.FromSlash(strings.TrimPrefix(d.URL, "/")))
  		if !strings.HasSuffix(d.URL, "/") {
  			dest = filepath.Dir(dest)
  		}
  		if err := copyTree(d.bundle, dest, ".bt"); err != nil {
  			return err
  		}
  	}
  	return nil
  }

  func (s *Site) copyStatic() error {
  	dir := filepath.Join(s.root, "static")
  	if _, err := os.Stat(dir); errors.Is(err, fs.ErrNotExist) {
  		return nil
  	}
  	return copyTree(dir, s.opts.Out, "")
  }
  ```

  #copy a tree{
    `copyTree` walks a directory and reproduces it under another, file by file; `skip` names an extension to
    leave behind, which for a bundle is the `.bt` source itself. Copying streams through `io.Copy`, so a large
    video in a bundle is never held in memory, and the error from closing the new file is returned, because
    that is when a full disk is reported.

    ```
    // copyTree copies the files under src to dst, except those with extension skip.
    func copyTree(src, dst, skip string) error {
    	return filepath.WalkDir(src, func(p string, d fs.DirEntry, err error) error {
    		if err != nil {
    			return err
    		}
    		name := d.Name()
    		if d.IsDir() {
    			if name == ".git" || name == ".hg" || name == ".svn" {
    				return filepath.SkipDir
    			}
    			return nil
    		}
    		if name == ".DS_Store" || (skip != "" && filepath.Ext(name) == skip) {
    			return nil
    		}
    		rel, err := filepath.Rel(src, p)
    		if err != nil {
    			return err
    		}
    		return copyFile(p, filepath.Join(dst, rel))
    	})
    }

    func copyFile(src, dst string) error {
    	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(dst), 0o755); err != nil {
    		return err
    	}
    	in, err := os.Open(src)
    	if err != nil {
    		return err
    	}
    	defer in.Close()
    	out, err := os.Create(dst)
    	if err != nil {
    		return err
    	}
    	if _, err := io.Copy(out, in); err != nil {
    		out.Close()
    		return err
    	}
    	return out.Close()
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Checking links}

#check links{
  After writing, barkdown reads every HTML file it produced and follows each `href` and `src` that stays inside the
  site. A link to a missing file is reported, and so is a link to a missing fragment, which catches a renamed
  heading that other posts still point to. Links to other sites are not fetched: a build should not depend on
  the network. The regular expressions are enough here because barkdown wrote this HTML itself and always quotes
  attributes with double quotes.

  ```
  var (
  	linkAttr = regexp.MustCompile(`\s(?:href|src)="([^"]*)"`)
  	idAttr   = regexp.MustCompile(`\sid="([^"]*)"`)
  )

  func (s *Site) checkLinks() {
  	ids := map[string]map[string]bool{}
  	idsOf := func(file string) map[string]bool {
  		if m, ok := ids[file]; ok {
  			return m
  		}
  		m := map[string]bool{}
  		data, _ := os.ReadFile(file)
  		for _, x := range idAttr.FindAllSubmatch(data, -1) {
  			m[html.UnescapeString(string(x[1]))] = true
  		}
  		ids[file] = m
  		return m
  	}
  	seen := map[string]bool{}
  	filepath.WalkDir(s.opts.Out, func(p string, d fs.DirEntry, err error) error {
  		if err != nil || d.IsDir() || filepath.Ext(p) != ".html" {
  			return nil
  		}
  		data, err := os.ReadFile(p)
  		if err != nil {
  			return nil
  		}
  		rel, _ := filepath.Rel(s.opts.Out, p)
  		for _, m := range linkAttr.FindAllSubmatch(data, -1) {
  			link := html.UnescapeString(string(m[1]))
  			target, frag, check := s.resolveLink(p, link)
  			var problem string
  			switch {
  			case !check:
  				continue
  			case target == "":
  				problem = "broken link"
  			case frag != "" && filepath.Ext(target) == ".html" && !idsOf(target)[frag]:
  				problem = "link to a missing anchor"
  			default:
  				continue
  			}
  			if key := rel + " " + link; !seen[key] {
  				seen[key] = true
  				s.warnf("%s: %s %s", filepath.ToSlash(rel), problem, link)
  			}
  		}
  		return nil
  	})
  }
  ```

  #resolve a link{
    `resolveLink` maps a link to the file it names. It returns `check` false for links it does not check, and
    an empty file name for a link that leads nowhere, including an absolute path outside the base path, which
    on the real server would leave the site. A directory stands for its `index.html`.

    ```
    func (s *Site) resolveLink(page, link string) (file, frag string, check bool) {
    	if link == "" || isExternal(link) || strings.HasPrefix(link, "data:") || strings.HasPrefix(link, "javascript:") {
    		return "", "", false
    	}
    	if i := strings.IndexByte(link, '#'); i >= 0 {
    		link, frag = link[:i], link[i+1:]
    	}
    	if i := strings.IndexByte(link, '?'); i >= 0 {
    		link = link[:i]
    	}
    	if link == "" {
    		return page, frag, frag != ""
    	}
    	if u, err := url.PathUnescape(link); err == nil {
    		link = u
    	}
    	var p string
    	switch {
    	case strings.HasPrefix(link, s.basePath):
    		p = filepath.Join(s.opts.Out, filepath.FromSlash(strings.TrimPrefix(link, s.basePath)))
    	case strings.HasPrefix(link, "/"):
    		return "", frag, true
    	default:
    		p = filepath.Join(filepath.Dir(page), filepath.FromSlash(link))
    	}
    	info, err := os.Stat(p)
    	if err == nil && info.IsDir() {
    		p = filepath.Join(p, "index.html")
    		_, err = os.Stat(p)
    	}
    	if err != nil {
    		return "", frag, true
    	}
    	return p, frag, true
    }
    ```
  }
}
#h1{The theme}

The built-in theme is a set of `html/template` files, a stylesheet, a script and an icon. Everything here can be
replaced per site by a file of the same name in `templates/`.

#h2{The idea}

The theme follows the page design of Edward Tufte's books #cite{tufte2006beautiful}, as Dave Liepmann's Tufte
CSS translated it to the web #cite{tuftecss}. Its proportions and markup are borrowed from there; the stylesheet
itself is written for barkdown's own HTML.

- The text sits in a column a little over half the width of the page, and the wide right margin holds the
  notes, numbered sidenotes beside the line they belong to, along with figure captions, the labels of code
  blocks, the table of contents, and on the home page each post's date and tags.
- The page is the faintly warm off-white of Tufte CSS, `#fffff8`, with near-black text; links are the colour of
  the text with a thin underline. Nothing else is coloured, apart from a muted palette for code.
- The typeface is ET Book, the free cut of Bembo made for Tufte's books, falling back to Palatino. Headings
  below the title are italic and regular weight. Small capitals open a new thought.
- There are no boxes, cards or shadows, and only a few rules: space and the column do the work.

In the evening the lamp can be dimmed: the page becomes `#151515` with light grey text, as in Tufte CSS's own
dark mode.

#h2{Layout}

#theme/base.html{
  The layout carries what every page needs in its `head`: the title, description, canonical URL, feed links,
  Open Graph tags for link previews, the ET Book faces when `#fonts` names a folder, and the stylesheet. A
  one-line script applies a saved lamp setting before the first paint, and marks the page as scripted so that the
  lamp button, which needs the script, is only shown when it will work. MathJax and Mermaid are loaded only by
  pages that use them.

  ```
  {{define "base"}}<!doctype html>
  <html lang="{{.Site.Language}}">
  <head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>{{if .Title}}{{.Title}}, {{end}}{{.Site.Title}}</title>
  <meta name="description" content="{{.Description}}">
  <meta name="generator" content="barkdown">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#fffff8" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#151515" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)">
  <link rel="canonical" href="{{abs .URL}}">
  <link rel="alternate" type="application/atom+xml" title="{{.Site.Title}}" href="{{url "/feed.xml"}}">
  <link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="{{.Site.Title}} (RSS)" href="{{url "/rss.xml"}}">
  {{- if or (eq .Kind "timeline") (and .Doc .Doc.Thoughts)}}
  <link rel="alternate" type="application/atom+xml" title="{{.Site.Title}}: thoughts" href="{{url "/timeline/feed.xml"}}">
  {{- end}}
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{url "/favicon.svg"}}">
  {{- with .Site.Fonts}}
  <style>
  @font-face { font-family: "et-book"; src: url("{{.}}/et-book-roman-old-style-figures/et-book-roman-old-style-figures.woff") format("woff"); font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-display: swap; }
  @font-face { font-family: "et-book"; src: url("{{.}}/et-book-display-italic-old-style-figures/et-book-display-italic-old-style-figures.woff") format("woff"); font-weight: normal; font-style: italic; font-display: swap; }
  @font-face { font-family: "et-book"; src: url("{{.}}/et-book-bold-line-figures/et-book-bold-line-figures.woff") format("woff"); font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-display: swap; }
  </style>
  {{- end}}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{asset "style.css"}}">
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="{{.Site.Title}}">
  <meta property="og:title" content="{{or .Title .Site.Title}}">
  <meta property="og:description" content="{{.Description}}">
  <meta property="og:url" content="{{abs .URL}}">
  <meta property="og:type" content="{{if eq .Kind "post"}}article{{else}}website{{end}}">
  {{- with .Doc}}{{if eq .Kind "post"}}
  <meta property="article:published_time" content="{{rfc3339 .Date}}">
  {{- range .Tags}}
  <meta property="article:tag" content="{{.}}">
  {{- end}}{{end}}{{end}}
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
  <script>document.documentElement.classList.add("js");try{var t=localStorage.getItem("theme");if(t)document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme",t)}catch(e){}</script>
  {{- if .Math}}
  <script id="MathJax-script" async src="{{.Site.MathJax}}"></script>
  {{- end}}
  {{- if .Mermaid}}
  <script type="module">import mermaid from "{{.Site.Mermaid}}";var d=document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-theme")||(matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches?"dark":"light");mermaid.initialize({startOnLoad:true,theme:d==="dark"?"dark":"neutral"});</script>
  {{- end}}
  </head>
  <body class="kind-{{.Kind}}"{{if .Site.LiveReload}} data-livereload="{{url "/__livereload"}}"{{end}}>
  <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to the writing</a>
  <header class="masthead">
  <div class="wrap">
  <p class="brand"><a href="{{url "/"}}">{{.Site.Title}}</a></p>
  {{with .Site.Subtitle}}<p class="tagline">{{.}}</p>{{end}}
  <nav class="menu" aria-label="Site">
  {{- range .Site.Menu}}
  <a href="{{url .URL}}"{{if eq .URL $.URL}} aria-current="page"{{end}}>{{.Title}}</a>
  {{- end}}
  <button class="lamp" type="button">Dim the lamp</button>
  </nav>
  </div>
  </header>
  <main id="main" class="wrap">
  {{template "content" .}}
  </main>
  <footer class="colophon">
  <div class="wrap">
  <p>{{if .Site.Footer}}{{.Site.Footer}}{{else}}{{or .Site.Author .Site.Title}}, {{(now).Year}}.{{end}}</p>
  <p>Follow along with the <a href="{{url "/feed.xml"}}">Atom feed</a> or <a href="{{url "/rss.xml"}}">RSS</a>.{{if not .Site.Footer}} Made with barkdown.{{end}}</p>
  </div>
  </footer>
  <script src="{{asset "site.js"}}" defer></script>
  </body>
  </html>
  {{end}}
  ```
}

#theme/partials.html{
  Three pieces are shared. `tagwords` lists tags as words separated by commas. `meta` is the sentence under a
  title; for a post with thoughts it also counts them and links to the latest. `summary` is one post on a list page: its title, then its excerpt with the date and tags set as a margin
  note beside it.

  ```
  {{define "tagwords"}}{{range $i, $t := .}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}<a href="{{url $t.URL}}">{{$t.Name}}</a>{{end}}{{end}}

  {{define "meta"}}<p class="meta"><time datetime="{{iso .Date}}">{{date .Date}}</time>
  {{- if and (not .Thoughts) (gt .Updated.Unix .Date.Unix)}}, revised <time datetime="{{iso .Updated}}">{{date .Updated}}</time>{{end}}
  {{- with .Author}}, by {{.}}{{end}}. About {{.ReadingTime}} minute{{if ne .ReadingTime 1}}s{{end}} to read.
  {{- with .Latest}} {{len $.Thoughts}} thought{{if ne (len $.Thoughts) 1}}s{{end}} so far, the latest on <a href="#{{.ID}}">{{stamp .}}</a>.{{end}}
  {{- if .Draft}} <span class="draft">This is a draft.</span>{{end}}</p>{{end}}

  {{define "summary"}}<article class="entry">
  <h2><a href="{{url .URL}}">{{.Title}}</a>{{if .Draft}} <span class="draft">draft</span>{{end}}</h2>
  <div class="entry-body">
  <span class="marginnote entry-note"><time datetime="{{iso .Date}}">{{date .Date}}</time>{{with .Thoughts}}<br>{{len .}} thought{{if ne (len .) 1}}s{{end}}{{end}}{{with .TagRefs}}<br>Filed under {{template "tagwords" .}}{{end}}</span>
  {{.Excerpt}}
  <p class="more"><a href="{{url .URL}}">{{if .HasMore}}Keep reading{{else}}Read the post{{end}}<span class="visually-hidden">: {{.Title}}</span></a></p>
  </div>
  </article>{{end}}
  ```
}

#h2{Pages}

#theme/list.html{
  The list template serves the home page, its continuation pages and the tag pages.

  ```
  {{define "content"}}
  {{- if eq .Kind "tag"}}
  <header class="page-header"><h1>Filed under {{.Tag.Name}}</h1>
  <p class="meta">{{len .Posts}} post{{if ne (len .Posts) 1}}s{{end}}. <a href="{{url "/tags/"}}">See every tag.</a></p></header>
  {{- end}}
  {{if .Posts}}<div class="entries">
  {{range .Posts}}{{template "summary" .}}
  {{end}}</div>
  {{- else}}<p class="empty">No posts yet. Add a file to <code>posts/</code> and build again.</p>{{end}}
  {{with .Pager}}{{if gt .Total 1}}<nav class="pager" aria-label="Pages">
  {{- if .Newer}}<a href="{{url .Newer}}" rel="prev">Newer posts</a>{{else}}<span></span>{{end}}
  <span class="pager-where">Page {{.Number}} of {{.Total}}</span>
  {{- if .Older}}<a href="{{url .Older}}" rel="next">Older posts</a>{{else}}<span></span>{{end}}</nav>{{end}}{{end}}
  {{end}}
  ```
}

#theme/post.html{
  A post ends with what a reader might want next: where it is filed, posts on the same subjects, and the posts
  written just before and after it.

  ```
  {{define "content"}}{{with .Doc}}
  <article class="post">
  <header class="post-header">
  <h1>{{.Title}}</h1>
  {{with .Subtitle}}<p class="subtitle">{{.}}</p>{{end}}
  {{template "meta" .}}
  </header>
  <div class="content">
  {{.Content}}
  </div>
  <footer class="post-foot">
  {{with .TagRefs}}<p class="filed">Filed under {{template "tagwords" .}}.</p>{{end}}
  {{if .Related}}<section class="related"><h2>More on the same subjects</h2><ul>
  {{range .Related}}<li><a href="{{url .URL}}">{{.Title}}</a>, <time datetime="{{iso .Date}}">{{date .Date}}</time></li>
  {{end}}</ul></section>{{end}}
  {{if or .Prev .Next}}<nav class="prevnext" aria-label="Neighbouring posts">
  {{with .Prev}}<p>Before this, <a href="{{url .URL}}" rel="prev">{{.Title}}</a></p>{{end}}
  {{with .Next}}<p>After this, <a href="{{url .URL}}" rel="next">{{.Title}}</a></p>{{end}}
  </nav>{{end}}
  </footer>
  </article>
  {{end}}{{end}}
  ```
}

#theme/page.html{
  ```
  {{define "content"}}{{with .Doc}}
  <article class="post page">
  <header class="post-header"><h1>{{.Title}}</h1>{{with .Subtitle}}<p class="subtitle">{{.}}</p>{{end}}</header>
  <div class="content">
  {{.Content}}
  </div>
  </article>
  {{end}}{{end}}
  ```
}

#theme/timeline.html{
  The timeline page lists the posts that grow by thoughts, the most recently added to first, each with its
  latest thought quoted in full and a link to read it in place. In the margin, as on the home page, stand the
  number of thoughts and when the latest was added.

  ```
  {{define "content"}}<header class="page-header"><h1>Timeline</h1>
  <p class="meta">Posts that grow a thought at a time, the most recently added to first. <a href="{{url "/timeline/feed.xml"}}">Follow every new thought with its own feed.</a></p></header>
  {{if .Posts}}<div class="entries">
  {{range .Posts}}{{$d := .}}<article class="entry">
  <h2><a href="{{url $d.URL}}">{{$d.Title}}</a></h2>
  <div class="entry-body">
  <span class="marginnote entry-note">{{len $d.Thoughts}} thought{{if ne (len $d.Thoughts) 1}}s{{end}}{{with $d.Latest}}<br>the latest on <time datetime="{{rfc3339 .Time}}">{{stamp .}}</time>{{end}}</span>
  {{with $d.Latest}}<blockquote class="latest-thought">
  {{.HTML}}</blockquote>
  <p class="more"><a href="{{url $d.URL}}#{{.ID}}">Read it in place</a>, or <a href="{{url $d.URL}}">the whole post</a>.</p>{{end}}
  </div>
  </article>
  {{end}}</div>
  {{- else}}<p class="empty">No thoughts yet. Add <code>#thought{2026-11-11 09:30 ...}</code> to any post to start one.</p>{{end}}
  {{end}}
  ```
}

#theme/tags.html{
  ```
  {{define "content"}}<header class="page-header"><h1>Tags</h1></header>
  {{if .Site.Tags}}<ul class="tag-index">
  {{range .Site.Tags}}<li><a href="{{url .URL}}">{{.Name}}</a> <span class="count">{{len .Posts}}</span></li>
  {{end}}</ul>{{else}}<p class="empty">No tags yet. Add <code>#tags{...}</code> to a post to file it.</p>{{end}}
  {{end}}
  ```
}

#theme/archive.html{
  ```
  {{define "content"}}<header class="page-header"><h1>Archive</h1><p class="meta">Everything written here, newest first.</p></header>
  {{range .Years}}<section class="year"><h2>{{.Year}}</h2><ul class="archive">
  {{range .Posts}}<li><time datetime="{{iso .Date}}">{{.Date.Format "2 Jan"}}</time> <a href="{{url .URL}}">{{.Title}}</a></li>
  {{end}}</ul></section>
  {{else}}<p class="empty">No posts yet.</p>{{end}}
  {{end}}
  ```
}

#theme/search.html{
  The search form fails politely without the script: the `noscript` note points to the archive.

  ```
  {{define "content"}}<header class="page-header"><h1>Search</h1></header>
  <form id="search-form" class="search" role="search" action="{{url "/search/"}}" data-index="{{url "/search.json"}}">
  <label for="search-input" class="visually-hidden">Search the posts and pages</label>
  <input id="search-input" type="search" name="q" placeholder="What are you looking for?" autocomplete="off" autofocus>
  </form>
  <p id="search-status" class="meta" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div id="search-results"></div>
  <noscript><p>Search needs JavaScript. The <a href="{{url "/archive/"}}">archive</a> lists every post.</p></noscript>
  {{end}}
  ```
}

#theme/404.html{
  ```
  {{define "content"}}<header class="page-header"><h1>Nothing lives here</h1></header>
  <p>There is no page at this address. It may have moved, or the link may be mistyped.</p>
  <p>Try the <a href="{{url "/"}}">front page</a>, the <a href="{{url "/archive/"}}">archive</a>, or <a href="{{url "/search/"}}">search</a>.</p>
  {{end}}
  ```
}

#h2{Style}

#theme/style.css{
  The stylesheet starts with its tokens: daylight and lamplight as custom properties. Lamplight applies when the
  system asks for dark mode and the reader has not chosen otherwise, or when the reader dimmed the lamp.

  ```
  :root {
    --paper: #fffff8; --ink: #111; --ink-soft: #6b6b63; --rule: #cfcfc4; --mark: #f4ecc2;
    --tok-k: #7a1f1f; --tok-s: #2f5d2f; --tok-c: #8a8a80; --tok-n: #7a4f00;
    --tok-b: #2b4a6f; --tok-f: #111; --tok-v: #7a4f00;
    --serif: et-book, Palatino, "Palatino Linotype", "Palatino LT STD", "Book Antiqua", "TeX Gyre Pagella", Georgia, serif;
    --mono: Consolas, "Liberation Mono", Menlo, Courier, monospace;
    color-scheme: light;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root:not([data-theme="light"]) {
      --paper: #151515; --ink: #ddd; --ink-soft: #9a9a92; --rule: #3a3a36; --mark: #4a4220;
      --tok-k: #e3a3a3; --tok-s: #a9cfa0; --tok-c: #85857d; --tok-n: #e0c080;
      --tok-b: #a3bfe0; --tok-f: #ddd; --tok-v: #e0c080;
      color-scheme: dark;
    }
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] {
    --paper: #151515; --ink: #ddd; --ink-soft: #9a9a92; --rule: #3a3a36; --mark: #4a4220;
    --tok-k: #e3a3a3; --tok-s: #a9cfa0; --tok-c: #85857d; --tok-n: #e0c080;
    --tok-b: #a3bfe0; --tok-f: #ddd; --tok-v: #e0c080;
    color-scheme: dark;
  }

  <<<the page>>>

  <<<the column and the margin>>>

  <<<masthead and colophon>>>

  <<<list pages>>>

  <<<posts and pages>>>

  <<<prose elements>>>

  <<<code>>>

  <<<thoughts>>>

  <<<search and small screens>>>
  ```

  #the page{
    The sizes are those of Tufte CSS: the root size is 15 pixels and body text 1.4 of those, on a 2rem line. The
    page is 87.5% of the window, at most 1400 pixels, with an eighth of it as a left margin. Headings are regular
    weight; the title is roman, the others italic. Links take the colour of the text with a fine underline.

    ```
    html { font-size: 15px; -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; }
    *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
    body { margin: 0; background: var(--paper); color: var(--ink); font-family: var(--serif); font-variant-numeric: oldstyle-nums; }
    ::selection { background: var(--mark); }
    .wrap { width: 87.5%; max-width: 1400px; margin: 0 auto; padding-left: 12.5%; }
    p, dl, ol, ul, blockquote { font-size: 1.4rem; line-height: 2rem; }
    p { margin: 1.4rem 0; }
    li p, blockquote p, td p { font-size: 1em; }
    li ul, li ol { font-size: 1em; }
    li:not(:first-child) { margin-top: 0.25rem; }
    h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { font-weight: 400; line-height: 1; }
    h1 { font-size: 3.2rem; margin: 4rem 0 1.5rem; }
    h2 { font-size: 2.2rem; font-style: italic; margin: 2.1rem 0 1.4rem; }
    h3 { font-size: 1.7rem; font-style: italic; margin: 2rem 0 1.4rem; }
    h4, h5, h6 { font-size: 1.4rem; font-style: italic; margin: 1.6rem 0 1rem; }
    a { color: inherit; text-decoration-thickness: 0.05em; text-underline-offset: 0.1em; }
    a:hover { text-decoration-thickness: 0.1em; }
    :focus-visible { outline: 1px solid var(--ink); outline-offset: 2px; }
    img, video, iframe { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
    hr { width: 55%; height: 1px; border: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); margin: 2rem 0; }
    sup, sub { line-height: 0; }
    .anchor { margin-left: 0.3em; font-size: 0.6em; font-style: normal; color: var(--ink-soft); text-decoration: none; opacity: 0; }
    :is(h2, h3, h4, h5, h6):hover > .anchor, .anchor:focus { opacity: 1; }
    .skip { position: absolute; left: -999px; top: 0.5rem; background: var(--ink); color: var(--paper); padding: 0.4rem 0.9rem; }
    .skip:focus { left: 0.5rem; z-index: 10; }
    .visually-hidden { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; }
    ```
  }

  #the column and the margin{
    Every block at the top of a post's content is 55% of the page wide; headings, figures and the table of
    contents are the exceptions. A note is floated out of its paragraph by a negative right margin of 60% of
    the paragraph's width and is 50% wide, so it sits in the margin with a gap of 5% beside the text: the
    arithmetic of Tufte CSS. Because percentages of margin and width are both taken from the paragraph, the same
    rule works wherever a note occurs, as long as its block is 55% wide.

    Display math is the other exception: a long derivation may run on into the margin, as wide equations do in
    Tufte's books, rather than being cut off at the edge of the column. The table of contents and the labels of
    code blocks use the same space: the contents float into the margin
    at the top of a post, and a code block's language and chunk name sit beside its first line.

    ```
    .content > :not(h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6, figure, .toc, .embed, .math, .goto-toc),
    .post-foot, .page-header > p, .entry-body, .pager, .search, #search-status, #search-results,
    .archive, .tag-index, .tagline, .colophon p, .empty, main > p { width: 55%; }
    .content > ul, .content > ol, .content > dl { width: 50%; padding-left: 5%; }
    .sidenote, .marginnote {
      float: right; clear: right; margin-right: -60%; width: 50%; margin-top: 0.3rem; margin-bottom: 0;
      font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.3; font-style: normal; text-align: left; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline;
    }
    blockquote .sidenote, blockquote .marginnote { margin-right: -82%; min-width: 59%; }
    li .sidenote, li .marginnote { margin-right: -70%; }
    .sidenote-number, .sidenote-mark { font-size: 1rem; position: relative; top: -0.5rem; left: 0.1rem; }
    label.sidenote-number { display: inline-block; max-height: 2rem; }
    input.margin-toggle { display: none; }
    label.margin-toggle:not(.sidenote-number) { display: none; }
    .content > .toc { float: right; clear: right; width: 27.5%; margin: 0.3rem 12% 1rem 0; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.4; }
    .toc-title { margin: 0 0 0.3rem; font-size: 1.1rem; font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .toc ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.5; }
    .toc ul ul { padding-left: 1rem; }
    .toc a { text-decoration: none; }
    .toc a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
    ```

    The link back to the contents is pinned to the bottom right corner of the window, in the margin's italic,
    on a patch of paper so that it stays legible when text scrolls beneath it on a narrow screen. With the
    script it is shown only once the contents have scrolled out of view above; without it, it is always there.
    Scrolling to it is smooth, unless the reader has asked their system for less motion.

    ```
    html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
    .goto-toc { position: fixed; right: 1.5rem; bottom: 1.5rem; z-index: 5; padding: 0.25rem 0.7rem; font-size: 1.1rem; font-style: italic;
      color: var(--ink-soft); background: var(--paper); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 3px; text-decoration: none;
      transition: opacity 0.2s; }
    .goto-toc:hover { color: var(--ink); border-color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .js .goto-toc:not(.shown) { opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { html { scroll-behavior: auto; } .goto-toc { transition: none; } }
    ```
  }

  #masthead and colophon{
    The masthead is the site's name in the size of a title, the tagline in italic like Tufte CSS's subtitle,
    and the menu as a row of words in small capitals. The lamp is one more word at the end of the row, shown only
    when the script is there to work it.

    ```
    .masthead .wrap { padding-top: 3rem; }
    .brand { margin: 0; font-size: 2.4rem; line-height: 1.1; }
    .brand a { text-decoration: none; }
    .tagline { margin: 0.6rem 0 0; font-size: 1.5rem; font-style: italic; line-height: 1.3; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .menu { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.3rem 1.4rem; margin-top: 1.2rem; font-size: 1.2rem; font-variant: small-caps; letter-spacing: 0.04em; }
    .menu a { text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink-soft); text-transform: lowercase; }
    .menu a:hover { color: var(--ink); }
    .menu a[aria-current="page"] { color: var(--ink); }
    .lamp { display: none; font: italic 1.1rem var(--serif); letter-spacing: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); background: none; border: 0; padding: 0; cursor: pointer; }
    .js .lamp { display: inline; }
    .lamp:hover { color: var(--ink); }
    main.wrap { padding-bottom: 4rem; min-height: 55vh; }
    .colophon { color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .colophon .wrap { padding-bottom: 3rem; }
    .colophon p { margin: 0.3rem 0; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.6; font-style: italic; }
    ```
  }

  #list pages{
    On a list page each post is its title and its excerpt in the column, with the date and tags as a margin
    note beside the excerpt, so that the column holds nothing but writing.

    ```
    .entries { margin-top: 1rem; }
    .entry { margin-bottom: 3.5rem; }
    .entry h2 { font-size: 2.2rem; margin: 2.5rem 0 0.4rem; }
    .entry h2 a { text-decoration: none; }
    .entry h2 a:hover { text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-thickness: 0.05em; }
    .entry-body > p:first-of-type { margin-top: 0.8rem; }
    .entry-note { color: var(--ink-soft); margin-top: 1.25rem; }
    .entry-note time { font-style: italic; }
    .more { font-style: italic; }
    .draft { font-size: 0.55em; font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-soft); vertical-align: middle; }
    .pager { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; margin-top: 3rem; font-size: 1.3rem; font-style: italic; }
    .pager-where { color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .empty { color: var(--ink-soft); font-style: italic; }
    ```
  }

  #posts and pages{
    ```
    .post-header h1, .page-header h1 { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
    .subtitle { font-size: 1.8rem; font-style: italic; line-height: 1.2; margin: 0.8rem 0 1rem; }
    .meta { color: var(--ink-soft); font-style: italic; font-size: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0.6rem 0; }
    .post-header { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
    .post-foot { margin-top: 3.5rem; clear: both; }
    .post-foot .filed { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 1.2rem; }
    .related h2 { font-size: 1.6rem; margin: 2rem 0 0.6rem; }
    .related ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; font-size: 1.3rem; line-height: 1.8rem; }
    .prevnext { margin-top: 2rem; font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .prevnext p { font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0.3rem 0; }
    .archive { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
    .archive li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 5rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; }
    .archive time { color: var(--ink-soft); font-style: italic; }
    .year h2 { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
    .tag-index { list-style: none; padding: 0; columns: 2 12rem; }
    .tag-index li { break-inside: avoid; }
    .tag-index .count { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.8em; }
    .tag-index .count::before { content: "("; }
    .tag-index .count::after { content: ")"; }
    ```
  }

  #prose elements{
    Figures are as wide as the column and keep their captions in the margin, level with the top of the picture.
    Quotations are set in the column, an epigraph in italic with its attribution right-aligned in roman. Tables
    have rules only above and below and under the head, in the manner of a book. Call-outs, which Tufte has no
    use for, are reduced to an italic label and a thin rule.

    ```
    .newthought { font-variant: small-caps; font-size: 1.2em; letter-spacing: 0.02em; }
    .content > figure { position: relative; width: 55%; margin: 2rem 0 3rem; }
    figure img { display: block; }
    figcaption { position: absolute; top: 0; left: 110%; width: 50%; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.3; }
    .embed { position: relative; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; width: 55%; margin: 2rem 0; }
    .embed iframe { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; border: 0; }
    blockquote { margin: 2rem 0; padding: 0 0 0 2.5rem; }
    .content > blockquote { width: 55%; }
    blockquote p { margin: 0.8rem 0; }
    .by { font-size: 1.1rem; text-align: right; margin-top: 0.4rem; }
    .epigraph { margin: 3rem 0; }
    .epigraph blockquote { font-style: italic; }
    .epigraph .by { font-style: normal; }
    .epigraph cite { font-style: italic; }
    mark { background: var(--mark); color: inherit; padding: 0 0.1em; }
    kbd { font: 0.85em var(--mono); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 3px; padding: 0 0.3em; }
    abbr[title] { text-decoration: none; font-variant: all-small-caps; letter-spacing: 0.04em; cursor: help; }
    details { margin: 1.4rem 0; font-size: 1.4rem; line-height: 2rem; }
    summary { cursor: pointer; font-style: italic; }
    dt { font-style: italic; margin-top: 0.8rem; }
    dd { margin-left: 1.5rem; }
    .table { overflow-x: auto; margin: 2rem 0; }
    table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.6; font-variant-numeric: lining-nums tabular-nums; border-top: 1px solid var(--ink); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink); }
    caption { caption-side: bottom; text-align: left; font-size: 1.1rem; font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-soft); padding-top: 0.6rem; }
    th, td { padding: 0.35rem 1rem 0.35rem 0; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
    thead th { font-weight: 400; font-style: italic; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink); }
    .callout { margin: 1.8rem 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; border-left: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    .callout p { margin: 0.6rem 0; }
    .callout-title { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .math { overflow-x: auto; font-size: 1.4rem; }
    .flash, :target { animation: flash 1.8s ease-out; }
    @keyframes flash { from { background-color: var(--mark); } to { background-color: transparent; } }
    ```
  }

  #code{
    Code is set without a background, in the column, a little indented, as Tufte CSS sets it. A literate chunk's
    name stays where the original barkdown put it, just above the code. The language is written in the margin
    beside the first line, like a margin note, and the script puts the copy button in that same margin column
    under it, so the two can never overlap however short the block.

    ```
    code, pre { font-family: var(--mono); font-variant-numeric: normal; }
    :not(pre) > code { font-size: 0.8em; }
    .codeblock { position: relative; margin: 1.8rem 0; }
    .codeblock pre { margin: 0 0 0 2.5%; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.42; overflow-x: auto; tab-size: 4; }
    .chunk-label { margin: 0 0 0.3rem 2.5%; font-size: 1.1rem; font-style: italic; }
    .chunk-name { text-decoration: none; }
    .chunk-label + .code-head { top: 1.7rem; }
    .code-head { position: absolute; top: 0; left: 110%; width: 50%; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 0.2rem; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.3; font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .copy { font: italic 1.1rem var(--serif); color: var(--ink-soft); background: none; border: 0; padding: 0; cursor: pointer; opacity: 0; }
    .copy:hover { color: var(--ink); }
    .codeblock:hover .copy, .copy:focus-visible { opacity: 1; }
    .tok-k { color: var(--tok-k); } .tok-s { color: var(--tok-s); } .tok-c { color: var(--tok-c); font-style: italic; }
    .tok-n { color: var(--tok-n); } .tok-b { color: var(--tok-b); } .tok-f { color: var(--tok-f); } .tok-v { color: var(--tok-v); }
    .chunkref { font: italic 1.25em/1 var(--serif); color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none; letter-spacing: 0; }
    .chunkref:hover { text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-thickness: 0.05em; }
    .chunk { margin: 1.4rem 0; }
    pre.mermaid { text-align: left; }
    ```
  }

  #thoughts{
    A timeline opens with a thin rule and the number of thoughts, with the switch for their order at the right
    of the same line. Each thought's timestamp stands in the margin beside its first line, exactly where a
    sidenote would, and thoughts are separated by space and a short rule. On a phone the stamp moves above the
    thought.

    ```
    .timeline { margin: 2.5rem 0; }
    .timeline-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; gap: 1rem; margin: 0 0 1.8rem; padding-bottom: 0.4rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); font-size: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.6; font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .timeline-order { font: italic 1.2rem var(--serif); color: var(--ink); background: none; border: 0; padding: 0; cursor: pointer; text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-thickness: 0.05em; text-underline-offset: 0.12em; }
    .thought { position: relative; margin: 0 0 2.4rem; }
    .thought + .thought::before { content: ""; display: block; width: 3rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); margin: 0 0 2rem; }
    .thought > p:nth-child(2) { margin-top: 0; }
    .thought-stamp { position: absolute; left: 110%; top: 0.45rem; width: 50%; margin: 0; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.3; font-style: italic; }
    .thought + .thought .thought-stamp { top: 2.45rem; }
    .thought-stamp a { color: var(--ink-soft); text-decoration: none; }
    .thought-stamp a:hover { color: var(--ink); text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-thickness: 0.05em; }
    .latest-thought { margin: 0.8rem 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; border-left: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    .latest-thought p { font-size: 1.3rem; line-height: 1.85rem; }
    ```
  }

  #search and small screens{
    Below 760 pixels there is no margin to use, as in Tufte CSS. The rule that narrows the column is repeated
    with the same selector, so that it wins over the wide-screen rule rather than losing to its specificity: the column takes the whole width, notes are
    hidden behind their numbers (and margin notes behind a small circled plus), and tapping one shows the note
    under the line. Captions, code labels, the table of contents and the margin notes of list pages return
    into the flow of the column.

    ```
    .search input { width: 100%; font: italic 1.6rem var(--serif); color: var(--ink); background: none; border: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 0.4rem 0; }
    .search input:focus { outline: none; border-bottom-color: var(--ink); }
    .result h2 { font-size: 1.8rem; margin: 2rem 0 0.2rem; }
    .result p { margin: 0.3rem 0; }

    @media (max-width: 760px) {
      .wrap { width: 84%; padding: 0 8%; box-sizing: content-box; }
      .content > :not(h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6, figure, .toc, .embed, .math, .goto-toc), .content > figure, .content > ul, .content > ol, .content > dl, .post-foot, .page-header > p,
      .entry-body, .pager, .search, #search-status, #search-results, .archive, .tag-index, .masthead .menu, .tagline,
      .colophon p, .empty, main > p, hr, .embed { width: 100%; }
      .content > ul, .content > ol, .content > dl { padding-left: 1.4rem; }
      h1 { font-size: 2.6rem; margin-top: 2.5rem; }
      .brand { font-size: 2rem; }
      label.margin-toggle:not(.sidenote-number) { display: inline; }
      .sidenote, .marginnote { display: none; }
      .margin-toggle:checked + .sidenote, .margin-toggle:checked + .marginnote {
        display: block; float: left; left: 1rem; clear: both; width: 95%; margin: 1rem 2.5%; position: relative;
      }
      label { cursor: pointer; }
      .entry-note { display: block; float: none; width: 100%; margin: 0.4rem 0 0; }
      .content > .toc { float: none; width: 100%; margin: 1rem 0; }
      figcaption, .code-head { position: static; width: auto; margin-top: 0.5rem; }
      .code-head { flex-direction: row; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; margin: 0 0 0.3rem; }
      .code-head .copy { margin-left: auto; opacity: 1; }
      .codeblock pre, .chunk-label { margin-left: 0; }
      .thought-stamp, .thought + .thought .thought-stamp { position: static; width: auto; margin: 0 0 0.3rem; }
    }
    @media print {
      .menu, .colophon, .prevnext, .related, .copy { display: none; }
      body { background: #fff; color: #000; }
    }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .flash, :target { animation: none; } }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Script}

#theme/site.js{
  The script does seven small things: it works the lamp, shows the link back to the contents, flips timelines between oldest and newest first, adds copy buttons to code blocks (into the
  margin column where the language is, making one when the block names no language), runs the search page,
  replays the original barkdown's highlight animation when a reader follows a link inside the page (so that
  following a chunk link shows where you landed even if you were there before), and, under `barkdown serve`,
  reloads the page when the site has been rebuilt. Storage is wrapped in `try`, because browsers block it in
  private windows. The lamp's label always says what pressing it will do.

  ```
  (function () {
    "use strict";
    var root = document.documentElement;

    var lamp = document.querySelector(".lamp");
    if (lamp) {
      var dimmed = function () {
        var t = root.getAttribute("data-theme");
        return t ? t === "dark" : matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches;
      };
      var label = function () { lamp.textContent = dimmed() ? "Turn the lamp up" : "Dim the lamp"; };
      label();
      lamp.addEventListener("click", function () {
        var next = dimmed() ? "light" : "dark";
        root.setAttribute("data-theme", next);
        try { localStorage.setItem("theme", next); } catch (e) {}
        label();
      });
    }

    if (navigator.clipboard) {
      document.querySelectorAll(".codeblock").forEach(function (block) {
        var code = block.querySelector("code");
        var head = block.querySelector(".code-head");
        if (!head) {
          head = document.createElement("div");
          head.className = "code-head";
          block.insertBefore(head, block.querySelector("pre"));
        }
        var button = document.createElement("button");
        button.type = "button";
        button.className = "copy";
        button.textContent = "copy";
        button.addEventListener("click", function () {
          navigator.clipboard.writeText(code.innerText).then(function () {
            button.textContent = "copied";
            setTimeout(function () { button.textContent = "copy"; }, 1500);
          });
        });
        head.appendChild(button);
      });
    }

    document.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
      var a = e.target.closest && e.target.closest('a[href*="#"]');
      if (!a || a.pathname !== location.pathname) return;
      var target = document.getElementById(decodeURIComponent(a.hash.slice(1)));
      if (target) {
        target.classList.remove("flash");
        void target.offsetWidth;
        target.classList.add("flash");
      }
    });

    <<<show the link back to the contents>>>

    <<<flip a timeline>>>

    <<<search in the browser>>>

    var reload = document.body.getAttribute("data-livereload");
    if (reload && window.EventSource) {
      new EventSource(reload).onmessage = function (e) {
        if (e.data === "reload") location.reload();
      };
    }
  })();
  ```

  #show the link back to the contents{
    The link is shown while the contents lie above the window, and hidden while they are in view or not yet
    reached. An `IntersectionObserver` reports exactly those moments, so nothing runs while the reader scrolls.

    ```
    var contents = document.querySelector(".toc[id]");
    var back = document.querySelector(".goto-toc");
    if (contents && back && window.IntersectionObserver) {
      new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
        var e = entries[0];
        back.classList.toggle("shown", !e.isIntersecting && e.boundingClientRect.bottom < 0);
      }).observe(contents);
    } else if (back) {
      back.classList.add("shown");
    }
    ```
  }

  #flip a timeline{
    Each timeline gets a button that reverses its thoughts. The reader's choice is remembered and applied to
    every timeline on the site, so someone who likes the newest first sees them that way everywhere. The label
    says what pressing the button will do.

    ```
    document.querySelectorAll(".timeline").forEach(function (timeline) {
      var head = timeline.querySelector(".timeline-head");
      var order = timeline.getAttribute("data-order");
      var button = document.createElement("button");
      button.type = "button";
      button.className = "timeline-order";
      var label = function () { button.textContent = order === "newest" ? "Show oldest first" : "Show newest first"; };
      var flip = function () {
        var thoughts = Array.prototype.slice.call(timeline.children).filter(function (el) { return el.classList.contains("thought"); });
        thoughts.reverse().forEach(function (el) { timeline.appendChild(el); });
        order = order === "newest" ? "oldest" : "newest";
        timeline.setAttribute("data-order", order);
        label();
      };
      var saved = null;
      try { saved = localStorage.getItem("timeline-order"); } catch (e) {}
      if (saved && saved !== order) flip();
      label();
      button.addEventListener("click", function () {
        flip();
        try { localStorage.setItem("timeline-order", order); } catch (e) {}
      });
      if (head) head.appendChild(button);
    });
    ```
  }

  #search in the browser{
    Search loads the index once and matches every word of the query, case-insensitively, as a substring of
    the title, the tags or the text. A post must contain every word to be found. Hits in the title count ten
    times as much as hits in the text, and tags five times, so a post about a topic outranks one that mentions
    it. The query is kept in the address, so a search can be bookmarked or shared, and the result shows the
    text around the first word with that word marked.

    ```
    var form = document.getElementById("search-form");
    if (form) {
      var input = document.getElementById("search-input");
      var results = document.getElementById("search-results");
      var status = document.getElementById("search-status");
      var docs = [];
      var esc = function (s) {
        return String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g, function (c) { return "&#" + c.charCodeAt(0) + ";"; });
      };
      var count = function (hay, needle) {
        var n = 0, i = hay.indexOf(needle);
        while (i >= 0 && n < 20) { n++; i = hay.indexOf(needle, i + needle.length); }
        return n;
      };
      var snippet = function (text, term) {
        var i = text.toLowerCase().indexOf(term);
        if (i < 0) return esc(text.slice(0, 180)) + (text.length > 180 ? "…" : "");
        var start = Math.max(0, i - 70);
        var j = i - start;
        var part = text.slice(start, i + term.length + 110);
        return (start > 0 ? "…" : "") + esc(part.slice(0, j)) + "<mark>" + esc(part.slice(j, j + term.length)) +
          "</mark>" + esc(part.slice(j + term.length)) + (i + term.length + 110 < text.length ? "…" : "");
      };
      var run = function () {
        var q = input.value.trim().toLowerCase();
        try { history.replaceState(null, "", q ? "?q=" + encodeURIComponent(q) : location.pathname); } catch (e) {}
        if (!q) { results.innerHTML = ""; status.textContent = ""; return; }
        var terms = q.split(/\s+/);
        var found = [];
        docs.forEach(function (d) {
          var title = d.title.toLowerCase(), text = d.text.toLowerCase(), tags = (d.tags || []).join(" ").toLowerCase();
          var score = 0;
          for (var k = 0; k < terms.length; k++) {
            var s = 10 * count(title, terms[k]) + 5 * count(tags, terms[k]) + count(text, terms[k]);
            if (!s) return;
            score += s;
          }
          found.push({ doc: d, score: score });
        });
        found.sort(function (a, b) { return b.score - a.score; });
        status.textContent = found.length === 0 ? "Nothing here mentions “" + q + "”." :
          found.length === 1 ? "One post matches." : found.length + " posts match.";
        results.innerHTML = found.slice(0, 50).map(function (f) {
          return '<article class="result"><h2><a href="' + esc(f.doc.url) + '">' + esc(f.doc.title) + "</a></h2>" +
            (f.doc.date ? '<p class="meta">' + esc(f.doc.date) + "</p>" : "") +
            "<p>" + snippet(f.doc.text, terms[0]) + "</p></article>";
        }).join("");
      };
      form.addEventListener("submit", function (e) { e.preventDefault(); run(); });
      input.addEventListener("input", run);
      fetch(form.getAttribute("data-index"))
        .then(function (r) { return r.json(); })
        .then(function (index) {
          docs = index;
          var q = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("q");
          if (q) input.value = q;
          run();
        })
        .catch(function () { status.textContent = "The search index did not load. Reload the page to try again."; });
    }
    ```
  }
}

#theme/favicon.svg{
  The icon is a dog house with a hash sign for a door, in ink on Tufte's paper.

  ```
  <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 64 64"><rect width="64" height="64" rx="10" fill="#fffff8"/><path d="M12 30 32 13l20 17v21H12z" fill="none" stroke="#111" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M28 33v13M37 33v13M23.5 37.5h18M22.5 42.5h18" stroke="#111" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
  ```
}
#h1{The development server}

`barkdown serve` builds the site into a temporary directory, serves it over HTTP, and rebuilds whenever a file in
the site changes. Pages built for the server contain a `data-livereload` attribute; the theme's script then
opens a connection to the server and reloads the page when told a new build is ready. Drafts and future posts
are included by default while serving, since previewing them is the point.

#serve{
  The server mounts the site at its base path, so a site configured for `https://user.github.io/blog/` is
  previewed at `http://localhost:8080/blog/`, links and all; a request outside the base path is redirected
  into it. A build that fails keeps the previous build online and prints the error, so a half-typed tag does not
  take the preview down. A read-write lock keeps a request from reading a file in the middle of a rebuild.

  ```
  func serve(opts Options, addr string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) error {
  	host := addr
  	if strings.HasPrefix(host, ":") {
  		host = "localhost" + host
  	}
  	opts.Serving = host
  	if opts.Out == "" {
  		dir, err := os.MkdirTemp("", "barkdown-serve-")
  		if err != nil {
  			return err
  		}
  		defer os.RemoveAll(dir)
  		opts.Out = dir
  	}
  	var mu sync.RWMutex
  	reload := newHub()
  	var site *Site
  	build := func() {
  		mu.Lock()
  		defer mu.Unlock()
  		start := time.Now()
  		s, err := Build(opts)
  		if err != nil {
  			fmt.Fprintf(stderr, "build failed: %v\n", err)
  			return
  		}
  		site = s
  		report(stdout, stderr, s, time.Since(start))
  		reload.send("reload")
  	}
  	build()
  	if site == nil {
  		return fmt.Errorf("the first build failed; fix the error above and run barkdown serve again")
  	}
  	base := site.basePath
  	go watch(opts.Src, opts.Out, build)

  	mux := http.NewServeMux()
  	mux.Handle(base+"__livereload", reload)
  	files := http.StripPrefix(strings.TrimSuffix(base, "/"), notFound(opts.Out, base))
  	mux.Handle(base, http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
  		mu.RLock()
  		defer mu.RUnlock()
  		w.Header().Set("Cache-Control", "no-store")
  		files.ServeHTTP(w, r)
  	}))
  	if base != "/" {
  		mux.Handle("/", http.RedirectHandler(base, http.StatusFound))
  	}
  	fmt.Fprintf(stdout, "serving at http://%s%s (Ctrl-C to stop)\n", host, base)
  	return http.ListenAndServe(addr, mux)
  }
  ```

  #serve the 404 page{
    A static host answers a missing path with the site's `404.html` and the status 404. The development server
    does the same, so the 404 page can be previewed by visiting any wrong address. Everything else is Go's
    standard file server.

    ```
    func notFound(dir, base string) http.Handler {
    	fileServer := http.FileServer(http.Dir(dir))
    	return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    		p := filepath.Join(dir, filepath.FromSlash(path.Clean("/"+r.URL.Path)))
    		if info, err := os.Stat(p); err == nil && info.IsDir() {
    			p = filepath.Join(p, "index.html")
    		}
    		if _, err := os.Stat(p); err != nil {
    			if page, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(dir, "404.html")); err == nil {
    				w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "text/html; charset=utf-8")
    				w.WriteHeader(http.StatusNotFound)
    				w.Write(page)
    				return
    			}
    		}
    		fileServer.ServeHTTP(w, r)
    	})
    }
    ```
  }
}

#h2{Watching for changes}

#watch{
  The standard library has no portable way to be notified of file changes, so the watcher polls. Every half
  second it walks the site and computes a fingerprint from the path, size and modification time of every file,
  hashed with FNV-1a. Walking a blog of a few hundred files takes well under a millisecond, which makes polling
  cheap enough. The output directory and hidden directories such as `.git` are skipped; otherwise every build
  would trigger the next.

  ```
  func watch(src, out string, rebuild func()) {
  	absOut, _ := filepath.Abs(out)
  	last := fingerprint(src, absOut)
  	for range time.Tick(500 * time.Millisecond) {
  		if now := fingerprint(src, absOut); now != last {
  			last = now
  			rebuild()
  		}
  	}
  }

  func fingerprint(src, absOut string) uint64 {
  	h := fnv.New64a()
  	filepath.WalkDir(src, func(p string, d fs.DirEntry, err error) error {
  		if err != nil {
  			return nil
  		}
  		if d.IsDir() {
  			abs, _ := filepath.Abs(p)
  			if abs == absOut || (p != src && strings.HasPrefix(d.Name(), ".")) {
  				return filepath.SkipDir
  			}
  			return nil
  		}
  		if info, err := d.Info(); err == nil {
  			fmt.Fprintf(h, "%s %d %d\n", p, info.Size(), info.ModTime().UnixNano())
  		}
  		return nil
  	})
  	return h.Sum64()
  }
  ```
}

#live reload hub{
  Live reloading uses server-sent events, a long-lived HTTP response in which the server writes `data:` lines
  that the browser's `EventSource` receives as messages. Unlike a WebSocket it needs nothing beyond
  `net/http`. The hub keeps one buffered channel per open page. Sending never blocks: a page that has not
  picked up the previous message does not need a second one.

  ```
  type hub struct {
  	mu      sync.Mutex
  	clients map[chan string]bool
  }

  func newHub() *hub { return &hub{clients: map[chan string]bool{}} }

  func (h *hub) send(msg string) {
  	h.mu.Lock()
  	defer h.mu.Unlock()
  	for c := range h.clients {
  		select {
  		case c <- msg:
  		default:
  		}
  	}
  }

  func (h *hub) ServeHTTP(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
  	flusher, ok := w.(http.Flusher)
  	if !ok {
  		http.Error(w, "streaming unsupported", http.StatusInternalServerError)
  		return
  	}
  	w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "text/event-stream")
  	w.Header().Set("Cache-Control", "no-store")
  	c := make(chan string, 1)
  	h.mu.Lock()
  	h.clients[c] = true
  	h.mu.Unlock()
  	defer func() {
  		h.mu.Lock()
  		delete(h.clients, c)
  		h.mu.Unlock()
  	}()
  	fmt.Fprint(w, ": connected\n\n")
  	flusher.Flush()
  	for {
  		select {
  		case <-r.Context().Done():
  			return
  		case msg := <-c:
  			fmt.Fprintf(w, "data: %s\n\n", msg)
  			flusher.Flush()
  		}
  	}
  }
  ```
}
#h1{Commands}

#h2{Starting a site and a post}

#init a site{
  `barkdown init` copies the example site of the next section into a directory. The example is compiled into the
  binary like the theme, so `init` works offline and the starting point always matches the program's version.
  It refuses to touch a directory that already holds a site.

  ```
  //go:embed example
  var exampleFS embed.FS

  func initSite(dir string, stdout io.Writer) error {
  	if _, err := os.Stat(filepath.Join(dir, "site.bt")); err == nil {
  		return fmt.Errorf("%s already contains a site.bt", dir)
  	}
  	example, _ := fs.Sub(exampleFS, "example")
  	err := fs.WalkDir(example, ".", func(p string, d fs.DirEntry, err error) error {
  		if err != nil {
  			return err
  		}
  		target := filepath.Join(dir, filepath.FromSlash(p))
  		if d.IsDir() {
  			return os.MkdirAll(target, 0o755)
  		}
  		data, err := fs.ReadFile(example, p)
  		if err != nil {
  			return err
  		}
  		return os.WriteFile(target, data, 0o644)
  	})
  	if err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	fmt.Fprintf(stdout, "created a site in %s\n\n  cd %s\n  barkdown serve\n\n", dir, dir)
  	return nil
  }
  ```
}

#new document{
  `barkdown new post "Title"` writes `posts/YYYY-MM-DD-title.bt` with the metadata filled in and `#draft{}` set,
  so that a half-written post cannot be published by accident; `barkdown serve` shows drafts, `barkdown build` does
  not. A title containing brashtag's special characters is put between backticks, using one more backtick than
  the longest run inside it. The spaces inside the backticks are harmless, because metadata values have their
  white space collapsed.

  ```
  func newDoc(src, kind, title string, now time.Time, stdout io.Writer) error {
  	slug := slugify(title)
  	if slug == "" {
  		return fmt.Errorf("the title %q has no letters or digits to make a file name from", title)
  	}
  	var path, text string
  	switch kind {
  	case "post":
  		path = filepath.Join(src, "posts", now.Format("2006-01-02")+"-"+slug+".bt")
  		text = fmt.Sprintf("#title{%s}\n#date{%s}\n#tags{}\n#summary{}\n#draft{}\n\n"+
  			"Start writing here. Remove `#draft{}` above when the post is ready.\n",
  			quoteBT(title), now.Format("2006-01-02 15:04"))
  	case "page":
  		path = filepath.Join(src, "pages", slug+".bt")
  		text = fmt.Sprintf("#title{%s}\n\nStart writing here.\n", quoteBT(title))
  	default:
  		return fmt.Errorf("barkdown new: make a post or a page, not %q", kind)
  	}
  	if _, err := os.Stat(path); err == nil {
  		return fmt.Errorf("%s already exists", path)
  	}
  	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(path), 0o755); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	if err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte(text), 0o644); err != nil {
  		return err
  	}
  	fmt.Fprintln(stdout, "created", path)
  	return nil
  }

  // quoteBT protects text that contains brashtag's special characters.
  func quoteBT(s string) string {
  	if !strings.ContainsAny(s, "#{}`") {
  		return s
  	}
  	run, longest := 0, 0
  	for _, r := range s {
  		if r == '`' {
  			run++
  			longest = max(longest, run)
  		} else {
  			run = 0
  		}
  	}
  	ticks := strings.Repeat("`", longest+1)
  	return ticks + " " + s + " " + ticks
  }
  ```
}

#h2{Converting a single file}

#render a single file{
  `barkdown render post.bt > post.html` is the original barkdown grown up: one document in, one self-contained page out, with
  the theme's stylesheet inlined. Without a file name it reads standard input, exactly like the original. There is no
  site, so `#ref` cannot resolve and site paths get no base path.

  ```
  const standalone = `<!doctype html>
  <html>
  <head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>%s</title>
  <style>
  %s</style>
  %s</head>
  <body>
  <main class="wrap">
  <article class="post">
  %s<div class="content">
  %s</div>
  </article>
  </main>
  </body>
  </html>
  `

  func renderFile(in io.Reader, name string, stdout io.Writer) ([]string, error) {
  	src, err := io.ReadAll(in)
  	if err != nil {
  		return nil, err
  	}
  	d, err := ParseDoc(name, string(src))
  	if err != nil {
  		return nil, err
  	}
  	r := NewRenderer(nil)
  	body := r.Render(d.body)
  	css, _ := fs.ReadFile(themeFS, "theme/style.css")
  	cfg := defaultConfig()
  	var head strings.Builder
  	if r.math {
  		fmt.Fprintf(&head, "<script id=\"MathJax-script\" async src=\"%s\"></script>\n", cfg.MathJax)
  	}
  	if r.mermaid {
  		fmt.Fprintf(&head, "<script type=\"module\">import mermaid from \"%s\";mermaid.initialize({startOnLoad:true});</script>\n", cfg.Mermaid)
  	}
  	title, header := name, ""
  	if d.Title != "" {
  		title = d.Title
  		header = "<header class=\"post-header\"><h1>" + html.EscapeString(d.Title) + "</h1>"
  		if d.Subtitle != "" {
  			header += "<p class=\"subtitle\">" + html.EscapeString(d.Subtitle) + "</p>"
  		}
  		header += "</header>\n"
  	}
  	_, err = fmt.Fprintf(stdout, standalone, html.EscapeString(title), css, head.String(), header, body)
  	return r.Warnings, err
  }
  ```
}

#h2{The command line}

#command line{
  Each command is a function from its arguments to an error. `run` takes its streams as parameters instead of
  using `os.Stdout` directly, so that tests can run the program as a user would and look at what it printed.
  Exit status 2 means the command was used wrongly, 1 that it failed.

  ```
  const version = "1.0.0"

  const usage = `barkdown, a static site generator for brashtag

  usage:
    <<<usage text>>>

  flags for build and serve:
    -src DIR       the site's directory (default .)
    -out DIR       where to write the site (default SRC/public; serve uses a temporary directory)
    -baseurl URL   override #baseurl{} from site.bt
    -drafts        include drafts (default for serve)
    -future        include posts dated in the future (default for serve)
    -strict        build: fail when there are warnings
    -quiet         build: print only warnings and errors
    -addr ADDR     serve: address to listen on (default localhost:8080)
  `

  func main() {
  	os.Exit(run(os.Args[1:], os.Stdin, os.Stdout, os.Stderr))
  }

  func run(args []string, stdin io.Reader, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int {
  	if len(args) == 0 {
  		fmt.Fprint(stderr, usage)
  		return 2
  	}
  	cmd, args := args[0], args[1:]
  	var err error
  	switch cmd {
  	case "build":
  		err = cmdBuild(args, stdout, stderr)
  	case "serve":
  		err = cmdServe(args, stdout, stderr)
  	case "new":
  		err = cmdNew(args, stdout, stderr)
  	case "init":
  		dir := "."
  		if len(args) > 0 {
  			dir = args[0]
  		}
  		err = initSite(dir, stdout)
  	case "render":
  		err = cmdRender(args, stdin, stdout, stderr)
  	case "version", "-version", "--version":
  		fmt.Fprintln(stdout, "barkdown", version)
  	case "help", "-h", "-help", "--help":
  		fmt.Fprint(stdout, usage)
  	default:
  		fmt.Fprintf(stderr, "barkdown: unknown command %q\n\n%s", cmd, usage)
  		return 2
  	}
  	switch {
  	case err == nil, errors.Is(err, flag.ErrHelp):
  		return 0
  	case errors.Is(err, errUsage):
  		return 2
  	default:
  		fmt.Fprintln(stderr, "barkdown:", err)
  		return 1
  	}
  }
  ```

  #flags{
    `build` and `serve` share their flags. The defaults differ in one respect: serving includes drafts and future
    posts, building does not.

    ```
    var errUsage = errors.New("usage error")

    type siteFlags struct {
    	opts          Options
    	strict, quiet bool
    	addr          string
    }

    func parseFlags(cmd string, args []string, stderr io.Writer) (*siteFlags, error) {
    	f := &siteFlags{}
    	set := flag.NewFlagSet("barkdown "+cmd, flag.ContinueOnError)
    	set.SetOutput(stderr)
    	set.StringVar(&f.opts.Src, "src", ".", "the site's directory")
    	set.StringVar(&f.opts.Out, "out", "", "where to write the site")
    	set.StringVar(&f.opts.BaseURL, "baseurl", "", "override #baseurl{} from site.bt")
    	set.BoolVar(&f.opts.Drafts, "drafts", cmd == "serve", "include drafts")
    	set.BoolVar(&f.opts.Future, "future", cmd == "serve", "include posts dated in the future")
    	if cmd == "build" {
    		set.BoolVar(&f.strict, "strict", false, "fail when there are warnings")
    		set.BoolVar(&f.quiet, "quiet", false, "print only warnings and errors")
    	} else {
    		set.StringVar(&f.addr, "addr", "localhost:8080", "address to listen on")
    	}
    	if err := set.Parse(args); err != nil {
    		if errors.Is(err, flag.ErrHelp) {
    			return nil, err
    		}
    		return nil, errUsage
    	}
    	if set.NArg() > 0 {
    		fmt.Fprintf(stderr, "barkdown %s: unexpected argument %q\n", cmd, set.Arg(0))
    		return nil, errUsage
    	}
    	if f.opts.Out == "" && cmd == "build" {
    		f.opts.Out = filepath.Join(f.opts.Src, "public")
    	}
    	return f, nil
    }
    ```
  }

  #subcommands{
    After a build, barkdown prints the warnings and one line of summary. With `-strict` any warning fails the
    build, which is what a deploy pipeline wants: a broken link or a misspelt tag stops the deploy instead of
    going live.

    ```
    func cmdBuild(args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) error {
    	f, err := parseFlags("build", args, stderr)
    	if err != nil {
    		return err
    	}
    	start := time.Now()
    	s, err := Build(f.opts)
    	if err != nil {
    		return err
    	}
    	if f.quiet {
    		stdout = io.Discard
    	}
    	report(stdout, stderr, s, time.Since(start))
    	if f.strict && len(s.Warnings) > 0 {
    		return fmt.Errorf("%d warning(s), and -strict was given", len(s.Warnings))
    	}
    	return nil
    }

    func report(stdout, stderr io.Writer, s *Site, took time.Duration) {
    	for _, w := range s.Warnings {
    		fmt.Fprintln(stderr, "warning:", w)
    	}
    	skipped := ""
    	if s.Skipped > 0 {
    		skipped = fmt.Sprintf(" (%d drafts or future posts left out)", s.Skipped)
    	}
    	fmt.Fprintf(stdout, "built %s, %s and %s into %s in %v%s: %s\n",
    		plural(len(s.Posts), "post"), plural(len(s.Pages), "page"), plural(len(s.Tags), "tag"),
    		plural(s.pages, "HTML page"), took.Round(time.Millisecond), skipped, s.opts.Out)
    }

    func plural(n int, noun string) string {
    	if n == 1 {
    		return "1 " + noun
    	}
    	return fmt.Sprintf("%d %ss", n, noun)
    }

    func cmdServe(args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) error {
    	f, err := parseFlags("serve", args, stderr)
    	if err != nil {
    		return err
    	}
    	return serve(f.opts, f.addr, stdout, stderr)
    }

    func cmdNew(args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) error {
    	if len(args) == 0 {
    		fmt.Fprintln(stderr, `usage: barkdown new post|page [-src DIR] "Title"`)
    		return errUsage
    	}
    	set := flag.NewFlagSet("barkdown new", flag.ContinueOnError)
    	set.SetOutput(stderr)
    	src := set.String("src", ".", "the site's directory")
    	if err := set.Parse(args[1:]); err != nil {
    		return errUsage
    	}
    	title := strings.Join(set.Args(), " ")
    	if title == "" {
    		fmt.Fprintln(stderr, `usage: barkdown new post|page [-src DIR] "Title"`)
    		return errUsage
    	}
    	return newDoc(*src, args[0], title, time.Now(), stdout)
    }

    func cmdRender(args []string, stdin io.Reader, stdout, stderr io.Writer) error {
    	in, name := stdin, "stdin"
    	if len(args) > 0 {
    		f, err := os.Open(args[0])
    		if err != nil {
    			return err
    		}
    		defer f.Close()
    		in, name = f, args[0]
    	}
    	warnings, err := renderFile(in, name, stdout)
    	for _, w := range warnings {
    		fmt.Fprintln(stderr, "warning:", w)
    	}
    	return err
    }
    ```
  }
}
#h1{An example site}

The example site is what `barkdown init` creates, and the tests build it, so it doubles as a specification of what
a post may contain. It has five posts (one of them a draft, one a page bundle with an image), an about page and
a configuration.

#example/site.bt{
  The base URL is a placeholder; a real site sets its own, because feeds need it.

  ````
  #title{Barkdown}
  #subtitle{A small blog written in brashtag.}
  #description{An example site made with barkdown, a static site generator for brashtag.}
  #author{A. Writer}
  #baseurl{https://example.com/}
  #perpage{5}
  #footer{Made with #link{https://github.com/PratikDeoghare/brashtag brashtag} and barkdown.}
  ````
}

#example/posts/2026-11-01-welcome.bt{
  The first post shows the minimum: metadata, paragraphs, and the `#more` marker that ends the excerpt.

  ````
  #title{Welcome to barkdown}
  #date{2026-11-01 09:00}
  #tags{barkdown}

  This is the first post of a site made with barkdown. Posts are written in brashtag: plain text, with
  `#tag{...}` bags where structure is needed.

  Write a paragraph, leave a blank line, write the next one. That is most of it.

  #more{}

  Everything above the `#more{}` marker is the excerpt shown on the home page. The rest appears only on the
  post's own page.

  #h2{Next steps}

  #ol{
    Run `barkdown serve` and open the address it prints.
    Edit this file and watch the browser reload.
    Read #ref{tour} for every tag barkdown understands.
    Run `barkdown build` and copy `public/` to any static host.
  }
  ````
}

#example/posts/2026-11-03-tour/index.bt{
  The tour is a page bundle: it is a directory with `index.bt` and an image next to it.

  ````
  #title{A tour of barkdown}
  #date{2026-11-03}
  #tags{barkdown, brashtag, reference}
  #summary{Every tag barkdown understands, shown in use.}

  #epigraph{
    Programs must be written for people to read, and only incidentally for machines to execute.
    #by{Harold Abelson and Gerald Jay Sussman, #cite{Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs}}
  }

  #newthought{This post shows} what a barkdown post can contain. Its source is `posts/2026-11-03-tour/index.bt`;
  read the two side by side.

  #more{}

  #toc{}

  #h2{Text}

  Paragraphs are separated by blank lines. Inside them you can write #b{bold}, #i{italic}, #em{emphasis},
  #code{inline code} or `inline code`, #mark{highlights}, #s{struck text}, H#sub{2}O, E = mc#sup{2}, keys
  like #kbd{Ctrl} + #kbd{C}, and abbreviations such as #abbr{HTML HyperText Markup Language}. A line
  break#br{}goes like this. The characters `#`, `{`, `}` and backticks go between backticks.

  Links take the URL first: #link{https://github.com/PratikDeoghare/brashtag the brashtag repository}.
  Links to other posts use their slug, like #ref{welcome}, and are checked when the site is built. A URL with
  a fragment goes between backticks: #link{`https://go.dev/doc/#learning` learning Go}.

  #quote{
    Let us change our traditional attitude to the construction of programs: Instead of imagining that our main
    task is to instruct a computer what to do, let us concentrate rather on explaining to human beings what we
    want a computer to do.
    #by{Donald E. Knuth, #cite{Literate Programming}}
  }

  #h2{Lists}

  #ul{
    - Each line is an item.
    - A line may start with a dash, as in Markdown.
    #ul{
      - A nested list sits on a line of its own.
    }
    - Items can hold #b{markup} and `code, with commas`.
  }

  #ol{
    First
    Second
    Third
  }

  The comma separated list of the original barkdown still works, and like every list it is a block of its own:

  #list{one, two, three}

  #h2{Tables}

  #table{
    Language | Typing  | First appeared
    ---      | ---     | ---
    Go       | static  | 2009
    Python   | dynamic | 1991
    #caption{Cells are separated by pipes; a row of dashes makes the row above it a header.}
  }

  #h2{Code}

  ```go
  package main

  import "fmt"

  // main says hello.
  func main() {
  	fmt.Println("Hello, barkdown", 42)
  }
  ```

  ```python
  def fib(n):
      """Return the n-th Fibonacci number."""
      a, b = 0, 1
      for _ in range(n):
          a, b = b, a + b
      return a
  ```

  ```sh
  # build the site and publish it
  barkdown build -strict && rsync -a public/ "$HOST:/var/www/site/"
  ```

  ```bt
  #title{A post}
  Some text with `#code` and a #b{bold} word.
  ```

  #h2{Notes and asides}

  Notes are written in place#fn{Like this one. It sits in the margin, level with the line that mentions it.}
  and numbered automatically#fn{On a phone there is no margin, so the number becomes a button: tap it to read the
  note.}. A margin note has no number.#marginnote{A margin note is for a remark or a caption that needs no
  reference mark.}

  #newthought{A change of subject} that does not deserve a heading can open with small capitals instead.

  #note{Call-outs come in six kinds: note, tip, info, warning, danger and important.}

  #tip{#title{A custom title}A #code{title} bag inside a call-out replaces its label.}

  #warning{A stray `}` in prose ends the document early. Barkdown reports the line.}

  #details{
    #summary{Click to see more}
    This stays hidden until the reader asks for it.
  }

  #h2{Figures}

  #figure{diagram.svg How barkdown works: a post is parsed into a tree, and the tree is rendered as HTML. The
  image lives next to the post, in its bundle.}

  #h2{Math and diagrams}

  Inline math is code between dollar signs: `$e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$`. Display math uses a bag:

  #math{`\sum_{k=1}^{n} k = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}`}

  Diagrams are Mermaid code blocks:

  ```mermaid
  graph LR
    A[post.bt] --> B[brashtag tree] --> C[HTML page]
  ```

  #h2{Raw HTML}

  #html{`<p class="center"><small>When nothing else will do, HTML passes through.</small></p>`}
  ````
}

